      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>ALWAYS USE cDOUGAL L'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOUR IS IS THE BEST.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>A more united trio than the Princess of Wales, the Empress Dewager of Russia. and the Duchess of Cumberland could not be. What is a grief to one is a grief to all, and the Empress has the deepest, truest, sympathy of her sisters in the loss of her son. The Duchess of Cumberland—like the Empress Dowager bas now had many years anxiety with regard to the health of her son, Prince George, but happily this is almost at an end, the Prince having of late grown considerably stronger and more robust in every way. The Czar of Russia has now to look to his younger brother, the Grand Duke Michael, who is twenty-one years of age, for the next heir to the throne. His three daughters are not wholly excluded from the succession, but all possible male heirs take precedence of them. On the extinction of the last male line the throne would pass into the female line,aceording to the degree of proximity to the Emperor who reigned last. This was the system of succession established by the Emperor Paul, and it is the same in Holland, Greece, and one or two other small States. Under the Salle system, which prevails in Belgium, Sweden, Norway, Italy. Denmark, and Prussia, women are completely excluded. In this country male heirs in the same degree of relation to the Sovereign exclude females without any regard to priority of birth. Thus, though the son of a Sovereign may be younger than a daughter, the son succeeds.- But if a Sovereign leaves a daughter, and a nephew the daughter eucceeds,as being in a closer degree of relationship. This is also the prac- tice in Spain and Portugal, and it is called the Cognate or Castile system. In this country, and in Denmark, there are four I generations of the Royal House in the direct male line now living. We have the Queen, the Prince of Wales, the Duke of York, and Prince Edward of York. In Denmark there are the King, the Crown Prince Frederick, Prince Christian who is twenty-nine years of age, and five years younger than the Duke of York, and Prince Christian's son Frederick who is an infant. It would also be scarcely possible for two Royal Houses to be more closely connected. Standing next to the res- pective Sovereigns are the Prince of Wales, the eldest son of Queen Victoria, and the Princess of Wales the eldest daughter of the King of Denmark. In the next generation, Princess Maud of Wales is married to Prince Carl, grandson of the King of Denmark. It ma' also be noted that the mother of the Prince of Wales, and the father of the Princess of Wales, are re- pectively the oldest Queen and King in Europe, but while the Queen has reigned longer than any other Sovereign new living, King Christian IX. of Denmark, did net ascend tbe throne till 1863. the year in whict. his eldest daughter was married to the Prince of Wales There will he the usual gathering of the differere branches of the Royal Family of Denmark next month, but there will be no festivities of any kind, owing first to the death of Queen Louise, and now that of the Cezarewitch. The Empress Etigese bee left Farnborough again, another cruise in her yacht being advised by the phys- icans. Her Majesty will be away until September, when, according to present arrangements, she will spend some weeks in Scotland. Not a day passes, when at Farnborough, without the Empress making a pilgri- mage, to the Mausoleum. This stands on a hill, and is guarded by white-robed, white-capped monks. The spot is an exquisite one, and none could wish for a lovlier in which to be laid to rest. Inside is a handsome altar, and by the side of the altar is the granite sarcophagus, containing the remains of the Emperor, and the Prince Imperial. Farnborough Hall, a typical English country seat, the home of the ex-Empress. stands close by. How delightful it would be not to have to observe fash- ion in dress in the hot weather. For instance,if we were allowed to pay a call in a loose muslin robe minus furbe- lows altogether, a huge floppy bat,trimmed with a knot of chiffon, and an airy fancy flower or two, and no gloves, what a happy state of affairs this would be all round. Other people, suffering as much as we from the discomforts of regulation dress, long to do this, but are deterred by the idea of what we would say. while we hesitate at the idea of what they would think. It is a deadloek. No doubt if the weather were to stay hot we should arrive at a comfortable compromise. but no sooner have we decided that come what may, we will no longer put up with this state of affairs, than—hey, preeto ! a change comes, and we are pursuing our warm tailor frocks,and a cosy fire,—and to again vanish all the good reselutions we have made. In the way of thunder storms we have been wonder- fully free this season, and those among us who have an antipathy to those particular functions have had good times. Compared with other European countries, our average of storms is very small but those who would escape them almost entirely should emigrate to Western Mexico, which favoured land has about one storm every three or four years. With us thunder storms generally show a tendency to run along river valleys, and their favourite time of day seems to be afternoon or evening, a morning visit from them being comparatively rare. The transparent frock, a slip of white washing silk, is the prevailing mode for small girls, and a very dainty mode it is. Picturesque styles too, are observed, yokes, and short waisted bodices with full sleeves and flowing ski' ts. being the principal effects. Piquant bonnets to co with this style of frock are being shown. They resemble in shape somewhat the well-known Dutch i}niicets, but are not quite so tight fitting, and have eeublea fluted lace edged frills in front. Mothers and nurses would do well to remember that it is absolutely needful f r a child to have its feet free. tor at least two or three hours every day, in order that the little muscles and sinews may have the benefit of natural exercise. Babies who wear shoes and stockings continuously have weak toes, and usually a flat foot. This nearly always runs into after life, so that the fort of the civilised man or woman is generally deformed and hideous. Those of Irish girls, who go barefooted until they are almost women, of Italian peasants, and of Arabian and Egyptian water-carriers, are models of beauty. Many advanced mothers are now allowing their children to run barefooted altogether,even in the streets. Dragon flies of glittering iridsscence, or white feather butterflies of the most exquisite lightness and trans- parency, hover on invisible wires over many of the best dressed beads, at the present time, and are a charming set off to a pretty evening toilette. For deep mourning, black specimens are to be had, but the effect is not by any means so good. A capital and inexpensive little hose suspinder is now to be bad at most of our large drapery establishments. 'this clips on to the cosset in the centre of the front,and serves the double purpose of holding the corset down and pulling one's hosiery up. It is a clever arrangement, ar d at once gives that graceful downward slope to the waist so greatly sought after just now. BROILED MWHROOMS.— Cleanse a dozen large mush- rooms by wiping them with a piece et flannel and a little salt. Cut off a portion of the stalk and peel the tops. Melt a piece of butter the size of a walnut in a saucepan, with a sprinkle of fl-ur, pepper and salt, then add the mushrooms, and cook five minutest Serve very hot on toast.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>HEWETT : "It is said that Mozart played the piano at the age of six." Jewett: "It's funny the neighbours let him live to grow up." MR. THRIFTY "Eight days without seeing you! It will be a long separation! Mrs. Thrifty: "Oh, don't worry. If I need money I'll write." " BRIDGET, this is altogether too much; you have a new follower in the kitchen every week." "Well, ma'am, you see, the food in this house is so bad that no one will come here for longer than a week." "THE doctor ordered your husband whisky for his rheumatism. Does it do him any good?" "He says it does him a world of good; but I notice the twinges come upon him more frequently than ever." MR. SCRAPPER: "When will you learn that razors are not the things to sharpen slate pencils with ? " "Mrs. Scrapper: " I don't know; I sup- pose as soon as you've learnt that hairpins are not the things to clean out pipes with." LITTLE 'Arry 'Opkins, who has just returned from a long tour abroad, says that the pyramids struck him with awe and constitution, while of Venice he remarks that the gorgonzolas on the water glided along like graceful piglets. (It is pre- sumed he meant cygnets). HARRY: "Say, old fellow, you ought to enter the golf tournament. This is the last day for the entries. l.'ve entered. I should say you had a good chance for second prize." Jack: How's that ? " Harry: "I'm the only one's that entered so far." "WELL, it was his Fate," said the pitying by- stander, as they carried away the bricklayer who had fallen off the roof. " Thrue for yez, sorr," said a sympathetic "mate." "He was sittin' on the copin' ateing his dinner, but he swung round to look at a foight, and, shure, the weight av thim fate av his pulled him over to the ground." THE GARDEN THAT I LOVE.—" Have you ever noticed," said one, "how fond people are of floral metaphors when they are speaking of a woman? Her cheeks are roses,' her lips are cherry ' ; her hands are always 'lily' hands; her mouth is a rosebud '•' her complexion is like a peach." "You've forgotten one," said the cynic. "What's that?" "Her tongue. It is a scarlet runner." MIGHT HAVE BEEN WORSE.—At St. Andrews an old farmer and his man were carting sand from the sea-shore. They were behind the target on the rifle-range, but hidden by a bank of sand from a party of volunteers who were at practice. A stray bullet struck the ploughman on the leg, and he immediately dropped, exclaiming, "I'm shot 1" The farmer scrambled hurriedly up the bank, and, waving his hands to the volunteers, shouted, "Hey, lads—stop that, will ye? You've shot a a man—and it micht ha'e been the horse !" Too READY.—How true it is that a guilty con- . .</p>
        <p>_ science needs no accuser is well shoWn by the • following true story. A gentleman went out of town for a day's fishing, taking his luncheon with him. When he reached the riverside, he discovered that he had dropped his luncheon somewhere on the way, and hastened back to look for it. By-and-by he met a burly tramp, who looked very well pleased with himself and the world. "Did you pick up anything in the road as you came along ?' asked the gentleman. "No, not me," answered the man of tatters. "I didn't pick up nothing. Couldn't a dog have found it and ate it up?" PREPARED FOR EMERGENCIES.—A clergyman, well known for his love of " horse-fiesh, ' was driving through a country village breaking in a new horse, when he overtook a doctor of his acquaintance on foot. "Jump in, doctor," he cried, pulling up. "I've got a horse here that is a perfect treat to sit behind." The doctor jumped in, and the parson drove off. The horse was "a treat" in the sense of speed and skittishness, and presently stood stock still, and shot both hind legs underneath the trap, splitting it to pieces and throwing out both occupants. The doctor jumped to his feet, feeling himself all over to see if he was injured. The man of cloth also got upon his feet. "Look here !" exclaimed the doctor, "what on earth do you mean by inviting me to ride behind a horse like that?" " I,Veli, you see," gasped the jovial cleric, "luckily there are no bones broken ; but when breaking in a beggar like this I like to have a doctor with me, you know I" THE SERVANT KNEW BEST. Cottle, in his "Life of Coleridge," gives the following account of the difficulties of the poets in an excursion. "I led the horse to the stable, when a fresh perplexity arose. I removed the harness without difficulty; but, after many strenuous attempts, I could not remove the collar. In despair, I called for assis- tance when aid soon drew Near. Mr. Wordsworth brought his ingenuity into exercise; but after several unsuccessful efforts, he relinquished the achievement as a thir'e, altogether impracticable. Mr. Coleridge now tried his hand, but showed no more grooming skill than his precedessors ; for after twisting the poor horse's neck almost to strangulation and the great, danger of its promi- nent eyes, he gave up the useless task, pronouncing that the horse's head must have grown—gout or dropsy—since the collar was put on; for he said it was a downright impossibility for such a huge os fronds to pass through so narrow a collar!' Just at this instant a servant girl came near, and understanding the cause of our embarrassment: La, master, said she, you don't go about the work in the right way. You should do it like this ; ' when, turning the collar completely upside down, she slipped it off in a moment, to our great humiliation and wonderment." A TEST OF ENDURANCE. —" What's the matter wi' you, old fellow?" asked Ferguson, as young Gray came into the smoking-room a few evenings ago, with an expression of deep anguish on his face. " Matter ? I've just had a tooth out," was the reply, with a groan, "and I haven't got over it yet. By Jove, how that fellow did hurt me !" "Fancy making all that fuss over the pulling of a tooth I said Mr. Joaker, with a laugh. "Why, some men go through a hundred times that amount of pain, without uttering a single sound." "Gammon !" ejaculated Gray. "It's a fact. I'll give you an instance," said Mac, solemnly. "My uncle was out shootimg big game in Dacota. He was aiming at a bison, when suddenly a bear lumped on to him, and knocked him down, growl- ing horribly. My uncle knew his only chance was to sham dead; and he lay as still as a mouse. Presently the bear began to chew his left leg; but my uncle never winced, though the animal gnawed and bit at the limb until it was reduced to a mangled, shapeless mass." "Do you mean to tell me that your uncle lay without uttering a sound while his leg was literally bitten to bits by a bear ? " cried Grey, incredulously. "It's a solemn fact," returned Mac. "Perhaps, however," he added, thoughtfully, " I ought to mention that it was a cork, leg." HER ADVICE.—" There is but one thing more." With an expression of intense earnestness on his frank and open countenance, the young man took once again the beautiful tapering hand that he had been holding, and gazed long and deeply into the eyes so lovingly fixed upon his. " Only one thing more," he continued, his voice growing richer and clearer as he proceeded. "Miss Clara, my dearest, the prompt manner in which you have responded to my avowal of love, to all those feelings which, having been locked up in my breast for so long, have at last broken their bonds, has filled me with the keenest joy; and now all that remains for me to do is to gain your father's consent; for, my darling, you do not know me if you think that I would consider an engagement as binding, without first gaining the consent of your parent. And yet, in view of your father's long absence —I think you said he would be away for thre.e weeks—tell me, dearest, if you think it would be entrenching too much on motives of delicacy, on that good taste, that respect for parental authority, for me, during the interval, to kiss you?" With an air of deep thought, which indicated but too plainly how keenly she appre- ciated the gravity of the situation, the young girl paused some moments before replying. "Of course, dear," she said at last, "you will have to decide the matter for yourself, and it would not be becoming in me to influence you in any way; but there is one thing that perhaps I had better tell you. In case you should decide this matter in the negative, I beg of you not to say anything about it. "Not say anything to him ?" he repeated, wonderingly. "Not tell him of the high and noble purpose that has animated me? Why not ?" "Because, dear," she replied, "if my father knew that you waited for three weeks to kiss me, he wouldn't think of giving his consent."</p>
