      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, Blouses are made of very light material indeed, snd some of the finest material I have ever seen has been upon several of my friends with whom I have been staying recently. Chiffonette is a sort of washing chiffon, which makes the prettiest of blouses and fronts. I saw a blouse of pale blue ehiffonotte which was made with a small square yoke of transparent white guivure. The collar was formed with a band of insertion, and the elbow sleeves had long transparent cuffs ot guipure. Nothing is more becoming than those elbow sleeves which form a long sleeve by arranging a sort of second sleeve of transparent lace or inser- tion reaching beyond the wrist, for it is a notice- able fact that our skirts and sleeves 'seem to be growing longer every day.</p>
        <p>Large hats are still the order of the day, but .are made as light as possible. Of course the good sense of having a hat of light material is easily recognisable, when we consider the almost tropical weather we have been experiencing lately. Brims have come back to the level again and are at the same time, fortunately, broad. The crown, is almost hidden behind a voluminous quantity of trimmings. Ribbon is still a conspicuous part of the hat and is made up in a a great variety of ways. Nor must not be forgotten the quantity of flowers, which still hold their own in the midst of the even varying fashions of the present day. I saw a splendid hat the other day on a lady who was out walking. The brim was up to date in regard to the shape and was of very light rice-white straw. The trimming consisted of electric blue ribbon affixed on the left side and a quantity of mar- guerites on the front. Along the underneath part of the brim were lying a quantity of marguerites.</p>
        <p>Amongst a large quantity of blouses which I have seen lately, those different to the muslin style have been composed of silk or nun's veiling. The large collars are useful to wear with simple little silk blouses fastened in front or at the left Bide. A very pretty fichu can be made of three narrow, scanty frille; this also forms an effective bodice trimming for cotton or muslins. The frills must be crossed into the waistband, and the skirt can be trimmed with three or five narrow flounces or frills at the foot. In very light materials such as white silk or nun's veiling, a pretty bodice can be made for a slight figure by gathering the bodice across rather fully, and finishing it off on the left Bide under a ruche of silk or lace.</p>
        <p>I may observe that tinsel trimmings are still to be seen in the shop windows, but they are very little worn by smart women, who prefer lace and gathered ribbons; but for evening wear lace—even real lace—is often seen spangled with tiny steel spangles. Checked and spotted materials, foulard, poplin, cashmere, and satin "mousseline "—a new fabric—are being much used just now. The im- possibility of having pockets in our dress has forced us to adopt small satin bags or reticules. Many women prefer a metal bag made in chain- work which fastens to the belt or chatelaine. These bags are exactly similar to the gold purses we have been using attached to our chains or chatelaines, but the "waist-bags" are much larger, and they can take purse, card-case, and hand- kerchief.</p>
        <p>Everyone should take care that their muslin robes are shaped as carefully as the most expensive material. They should set perfectly flat round the hips. The skirts are made in various shape.. Some have apron fronts, with graduated fluted frills joined on with a transparent lace insertion, others are made in the bell shape, and sonic have deep flounces at the bottom, with transparent lace insertions let in near the hem. Fancy hem- stitching is another favourite trimming, and it always looks well in muslin. These skirts are ready for wear, and only have to be mounted on a narrow waistband to the required length. The same idea is carried out in organdie muslins with small raised spots and fancy printed designs in soft colourings, and white or cream-spotted muslin robes, trimmed with tucks and insertions wad edgings of lace, are equally attractive. I have observed that the lace robes which have been prepared for fetes and race meetings are really exquisite, and they are made in various kinds of lace. The more elaborate models are necessarily rather ccistly, but considering how beautiful they are, and what a lot of handwork is required to complete an entire lace dress in the latest fashion, with a shaped skirt, sleeves, and bodice pieces, the prices are not by any means high. Your loving</p>
        <p>To MOTHERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their children while teething with perfoct success, It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste : it produces natural quiet sleep, by Nl:eying the child from pain and the little cherub awake-s "as bright as a buttoa." Of all Chemists is lid per bottle. —Advt.</p>
