        <p>G Lowe, Warrington "EJ P" Warrington R Mercer. Padgate 2 2 J Rosa, Penketh J R Grieve, Warrington 2 2 "C P," Warrington NOTlCE.—Solvers who become entitled to a prize in this competition can, if they desire, have any book on the game of Draughts equal to the value of 55,, instead of the cash prize by notifying the Draughts Editor.</p>
        <p>We have to announce that the winner of our last month's competition is Mr. C. Powell, (" C. P." Warrington), 7 :liarnard-St., Warrington, who has elected to have a book on the game in place of the ss. cash.</p>
        <p>The small diagram will enable our readers to understand the notation by which the moves of the game are recorded. Black occupies the squares num- bered 1 to 12 and white those numbered 21 to 32. The figures thus (11-15) indicate that a move is made from and to the squares so numbered. Black has the opening move and the pairs of figures apply alternately to Black and White to the close.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD METHODISM.</p>
        <p>"The Warrington -Circuit Wesleyan Methodist Church Record" says, one is inclined to think that the readers of these circuit jottings will have come to the conclusion that our work and life at Latchford is almost dead. But still we are glad to report (although we rarely chronicle our doings in the "Record ") that there appears a very bright and promising outlook for Methodism in Latchford. Our congregations are good, particularly the Sunday night services, which as a rule are exceedingly well attended. One is sure that to so such a gathering of young people, especially young men, who attend in good numbers, and who listen to the Word with rapt and careful attention, must be an inspiration to the preachers. Many of them have clearly awl intelligently consecrated their lives to God. Our payer if+ that they may be kept stead- fast in the faith—then we say the„future of Latch- ford is great. Old Established, Honest Dealers, SCALES AND SONS, 83. Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT SHEPHERDS, WARRINGTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the quarterly meeting of the above district was held at the Big Horse Shoe Inn, Horse- market-street, under the presidency of Bro. William Hall,who was supported by Bro. F S Massey (deputy chief) James Parr (treasurer),Peter Edgar (district secretary). Trustee Eckersley and delegates to the number of 20, representing the several lodges. The President,after a few words of welcome, called upon the Secretary to read the past quarter's accounts, which were found to be of a satisfactory nature. Substantial sums have been added to the funds of the district. The death claims were extremely small. After the auditors (Bros. Kirkham and Miller) had certified their correctness, the accounts were unanimously passed. A large number of new members were submitted to, and eventually passed by the district. Bro. Kirkham was re-elected auditor for another six months. The next business was the election of officers, examiners, &amp;c., for the year 1899-1901 Brother F S Massey was unanim- ously elected Prov. Chief of the district. After the arbitration committee had been elected Bro. Henry Warburton, at the request of the Chairman, gave a report of the A.M.C. proceedings; and, after a short discussion, a hearty vote of thanks was tendered to him Other matters affecting Shephedry were dis- posed of. The newly-elected officers were duly in- stalled, and the meeting closed after a hearty vote of thanks had been passed to the Chairman for his conduct during his term of office as Prov. Chief.</p>
