      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TfIE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 14, 1899.</head>
        <p>TfIE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 14, 1899.</p>
        <p>I THE "MADDING CROWD."</p>
        <p>[BY ." QUILP N.']</p>
        <p>It was with entlusias-ua that I -received the edi- torial commission, to spend last Saturday with Messrs. Walker's Employes,, toe occasion being ' their annual excursion, and the invitation to the "Examiner," being of the usual cordial descriptiou. I use toe word " enthusiasm" advisedly, because if there is a class ot individuals more subject to bore- dom than others, t should say it is the class known as the "pressmen." lam happy in being an excep- tion to thio rule—at times—and I rejoiced on this occasion. My anticipations of the morrow were not to be all pleasant though, for at a late hour the pre- vious night, the first shadow come over my golden dream as the dark thought suggested itself, that with my usual di,regard Nr the beauties of the early morning', I should oversleep myself. The thought plunged me into a cold perspiration for I had set my heart on this little jaunt. After much cogitation upon the merits of several wild schemes for awakening at 5 o'clock (unearthly hour !), the preparation of which would alone have taken ma into the small hours of the morning,l decided to let matters take their chance. My restless mind, however, did me good service for at a quarter to five I awoke to hear, not the sky- lark, but the long drawn yawn of a dog at the back. There is no sentiment about a yawn and I felt irri- tated beyond expression and inclined to throw ar- ticles of toilet at the dog for his want of feeling,but I reflected that he could not have read Ouida and therefore knew no better. Poor beggar !he must have been infinitely bored with his own company. Dismissing these thoughts, however, I made haste and eventually found myself at Bank Quay station in plenty of time for the train. As the time drew closer the crowd gathered in numbers until there were seven or eight hundred people on the platform waiting the arrival of the train. They were a good- tempered crowd for all that and the only ripple that appeared to disturb the calm surface of its tem- per was a newsagent. He made his way through the trippers with rather unnecessary energy carry- ing a heavy bundle of newspapers with either hand Some youth standing by, probably prompted by the thought that he was doing a public service,used his elbow with electrical effect oo the man's ribs. The end in this case, did justity the means, for the man pur- sued his way with more discretion and less danger to the little ones, not however, without directing most intemperate language and vague threats at me —'whom fate had made the unhappy scapegoat of the affair. Accompanied by the strains of the Borough Band the train steamed slowly out of the station and gathering speed as it went deposited us at the Central Station, Blackpool,after one of the quickest rens I had ever experienced in this direc- tion. The Warringtonians created a profound sen- sation amongst the few people who were up as they made their way out of the station to the inspiriting music of their band. As the excursionists were quite free to wander about until 10 am , when a special boat would convey them to Douglas, all went their several ways as inclination led them. Three members of the press who had come from Warrington, by a concensus of opinion decided to visit the Tower. towards which we directed our steps. It is funny how people break their usual habits when they make any stay at a watering place. They gat up at an impossible hour and parade .the promenade, blinking like owls, and on returning to their lodg- ings,look condescendingly, even pityingly, on their fellow lodgers who see no reason for changing their habits. This I advance, with due modesty, as a possible reason why so many people are more tired after a holiday than before. We duly arrived at the Tower without any adventures worth speaking of, and running the gauntlet of two or three indus- trious sweepers-up, who seemed to take a positive delight in work whilst we passed them, entered the snenagerie. A great pity for those noble brutes filled my soul as I watched them a safe distance from the bars. What uneventful lives they must lead seeing the same Lancashire faces gazing at •them day after day! Four lions we saw; three looked contemptu- ously at us as, children of nature that we were, we could not conceal our admiration from them. The fourth looked supremely bored and as we passed on our way out we saw him crouched at the extreme end of his cage, back humped up and staring with unseeing eyes; the apotheosis cf misery. Personally, I was qglad to get out of the place for I felt those eyes to be a reproach to the whole human race. We went out of the Tower passing on our way through the " Old English Village "in the streets of which thi4young lady assistants were putting the finishing touches to their toilets preparatory to charming the money out of the pockets of the visitors. A number of people decided to stay in Blackpool rather than proceed to the Isle of Man, in addition to others whose "prophetic instincts " warned them, despite the fairness of the weather, that the passage would be rough. By this time the opinions these estimable people held of their judg- ment, have probably suffered somewhat, as the sea was smooth enough for a rowing boat to make its way across ; and the sun shone with great brilli- ancy. As the Queen of the North" swung round from the pier-head and headed her bows for Douglas Bay,:the band struck up a lively air,and in contrast to a couple of young ladies, who were endeavouring to fascinate the gentlemen by their grace, two ladies who had long passed the meri- dian of life, gambolled through the witch- ing waltz, finally collapsing against the funnel in a state of excessive perspiration. Another:female in a half-hysterical condition from happiness and other things also distinguished her- self by displaying great activity in a three-act acro- batic entertainment on the saloon deck in which a bucket played an important part. However, the excursionists, generally, acquitted themselves ad- mirably and it only required the beauty of the scene as the vessel cleaved its way into Douglas Bay to subdue the excentricities of the lone female. The party had about three hours to "do "the island completely, and so far I have heard no complaints as to the inadequacy of the time. I admit one has his work cut out, so to speak, to explore all the beauties of the place, natural and artificial, but to a thoughtful man the task has difficulties but no obstacles. Guide books can be bought cheap full of detail and" descriptive" and a person with a retentive memory can display amazing knowledge of a place after a brief visit. One reporter whose identity I will not mention, wanted to drag us off to Grebe Castle and in the event of our eloquent tongues failing us, make Hall Caine submit to an interview by physical force. The plot was aban- doned, however, and it is as well, for as we looked more like brigands than respectable reporters, we should probably not have been allowed to enter the gate. After a wild rush to the boat, which nearly left, the exctilionists the I"ihnrfghter for their ramble on reshore pacing up and down the ended looking in all our calm, and the passengers, about, were very quiet, vessel.t ia from Thetheirs e a was walkingt their, still many drowsy. A party. of strolling players who the hours by playing on a discordant piano or had inflicted themselyes on our company enlivened harmonium—wbichit was I am still in doubt—and singing alleged comic songs. which had a saddening effect on most. But you will always find there is some congenial spirit who will come to the rescue on such occasions and on this particular occasion a Passenger—favouring the"harmsynious blacksmith" hisas he is powerful represented voice ain d withpictures—gave of a full play to band of followers who gathered round gave most praise- orthy and vigorous renderings of various songs and hymns, beating time in a vague manner with an emphatic looking stick. The other artistes were outgeneralled and retired disconsolate,but not alto- gether inconsolable as by reason of a naval looking uniform, and resplendent in gold braid, they were much admired by the fair sex. Multitudes lined the gangways on the pier to see the distinguished coin- pany—for our fame had gone abroad—and our re- entry into Blackpool was all that hauteur and our bandsmen could make it. Blackpool was once more at the mercy of Warrington but the men and women of the manufacturing town conducted themselves with great decorum and good spirit until a little before eleven o'clock, when our " special "left for home. Meanwhile some of the more favoured guests were entertained to a regal repast at the County and Lane Ends Hotel, after which the party proceeded to the station. As the train left the station and tore it way shrieking into the dark- dess, the band again r ‘newed our spirits with Divine melody, and a little before we got to War- rington a stop was made at a station, and gathering on the platform in a circle the Borough Band gave I a most finished rendering of "Lead Kindly Light." I Within a very few minutes more Warrington was reached, after a splendid run, the station being occupied by a host of friends and relatives of the excursionists anxious forstheir safe arrival.</p>
        <p>Police Uniform Thotg. SOAVES AND SONS, 83, Bricie-gtreet. Warrington.—Adir', J. BATLEY IsA.tc offers S MOIAL MINIS to SCHOOLS for his splendid PORK PIRS for Walkiag Day,—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE COUNTY</p>
        <p>ASSOCIA HON.</p>
        <p>INDIVIDUAL AIHRIT COMPETITION.—Tbie annual en- gagement was opened at mid-day on Saturday in the preience of a good attendance of spEc;ators. The green at Kersal Moor was in prime condition for play, and the weather most favourable. In the abs,nce of Mr. Alder- man Meakin. president. Mr. J W G Coombs, senior vice- president, formally opened the proceedings with a few opportune sentence 3. The total number of entrants was 139, among whom were nearly all the past champions. Coombs, P Fielding, and W Fielding passed successfully through the first round, but J C Howes and J William- son were unsucc,•ssful. In the course of the proceedings some singularly close finishes were noticeable, and in one instance when Wood, of Warrington, lay in for game with a couple of bowls, he unfortunately got in his opponent's line of play,ani impeded his wood,when the referee gave the win to Dtwoon, of Prestwich.Tnere was a fair show of runaway event's during the afternoon, a feature generally prevalent in the first round of a corn- ' petition. but little happened to call for special remark. Scores :</p>
      </div>
