      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>OBSTRUCTION OF WARRINGTON STREETS.</head>
        <p>THE OBSTRUCTION OF WARRINGTON STREETS.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. JOHN AND PATRICK MAGINN BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrin,guon Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, John Miginn, 114, Bridge-street, furniture dealer, Ec3., wa3 summoned for having on the 5b July caused a furaiture van to stand in Bridge-street for a longer time than was neces- sory for loading or unloading. Defendant pie3del not guilty, and said he claimed the right to keep the van there P.C. Cooper said that at five minutes to ten on Wednes- day morning he was on duty in Bridge-street, when he saw a furniture van belonging to defendant standing opposite Mr. Hill's shop, No. 111. It WAS there until 11.30, hut no loading or unloading took place. He asked the defendant how long the van would stand there, and he replied that it would stand there until it was loaded if it took twn days. Witness then told thin that the matter would be reported. The defendant then proceeded to cross-nx imine the witness, and observed, in reply to one of the answers, that he did not think the officer had any right to tell lies.</p>
        <p>The Mayor : We cannot allow that sort of thing here. Dgendant : Didn't I say I would load as soon as I could?</p>
        <p>Witness : Yes. Farther questioned, witness said he saw the defendant all the morning, but he only spoke to him once about the van.</p>
        <p>Defendant: I suppose it is no use cmtradicting him as he is on oath. If the Bench will postpone the case for half.an.hour, I will bring a witness to tell the truth.</p>
        <p>Mr. Maginn was informed that he had had plenty of time to prepare his case, and that the Bench could not wait the desired timm.</p>
        <p>Couacillor Shaw Green : You should have brought your _evidence. _</p>
        <p>Defendant : I came here on Wednegday to complain of my treatment—not me committing a breach of the law.</p>
        <p>Harry Whittles. in the employ of Mr. John Hill, said he saw the van from ten minutes to ten until twenty minutes to twelve. No furniture was put in it during that time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Magian asked for the case to be adjourned. Councillor Shaw Green : The van was there two hours, and yet nothing was done. The Chief Constable said he should be sorry to hamper Mr. Maginn or any one else in the matter of business, but the police had a duty to the public to discharge. Defendant: I have no doubt the van stood there an unreasonable time before we started to load. The Chief Constable : That is all we allege. _ . _ .</p>
        <p>Defendant: But I don's see where the—obstruction was. During the time the van was standing, two lur- ries of furniture had to be unloaded. I must either have liberty to transact my business or leave the tpwn.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable : I simply allege a technical offence. It is very painful to take these proceedings. The Mayor : We don't wis't to be harsh, and we shall dismiss the case on payment of costs. You must not imagine you can allow a van to stand in the street for two hoursland nothing b 3 done zo it.</p>
        <p>Patrick Magion, cab proprietor, Sankey-street, was charged with cassin,g an obstruction in Arpley-street by allowing a couple of omnibuses to remain therein. Inspector Taylor proved the case. He said he visited t'se defendant's stand in Arpley-street, and found there, near the stables, two 'buses and a number of other vehicles. Only two of the vehicles had horses attached to them, and an obstruction to passengers was caused. Some of the vehicles were afterwards removed.</p>
        <p>Defendant said he had nothing to say except that he occupied a somewhat public position and.everytbing he put out was seen. Some of the conveyances in the street may no have b3longed to him; plople had carts on the other side of the street. He admitted the 'buses were in the street. He hoped the police would not hamper him in his business. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said he was as reluctant to sum- mon Mr: Maginn as Mr. Maginn was to be summoned. Mr. Birchall : You cab proprietors do not seem to see that it is necessary for you to have more room for your business. It is the same with Mr. Connor. You are always complaining. This case was also dismissed on payment of costs,</p>
        <p>Defendant: We will do everything we can to pre- vent an obstruction, but the people ot Arpley-street don't want my business. The Mayor: We don't want to harass you. The Chief Constable remarked that the defendant carried on his business under great difficulties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CHESHIRE LINES ATHLETIC</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE LINES ATHLETIC</p>
        <p>FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>The twentv-sixtia annual festival in connection with the Cheshire Lines, the Midland, Great Central, and Great Northern Railways took place on Saturday at Knutsford. The sports were managed by a committee, of which Mr. F. C. Skillir.:orn was chairman and Mr. E. P. Connolly hon. secretary, and everything went off with the utunst smoothness and promptitude. There were large entries for the various events, and the prizes were both costly and useful. Results ;- 200 Yards Flat Race for youths under 17.-1. It T Ingham, Huskisson ; 2, J A Carpenter, Warrington ; 3, James Marsh, Warrington. 80 Yards Handicap Flat Race for employes over 40.-1, J Froy. Huskisson ;2, J H Abrams, Alexandra; 3, J Shaw, Warrington. Quarter-Mile Flat Race, Scratth.-1, E Husain; Canada. L N W; 2, Fred Johnson, Godley, G C; 3, J Cahill, Park- lane. L N W</p>
        <p>120 Yards Flat Race.-1, P H Marquis, Huskisson ; 2, W Blundell, accountant's office; 3, J Knowles, accountant's office.</p>
        <p>Two Miles Handicap Bicycle Race-1,8 R Marrow, Forth- wich, G 0, 160 ; T Sowerbutts,North Docks, L and Y, 240 ; 3, A Ball, Northwich, G 0, 220. Quarter-Rile Handicap Race.--1, H Buckley, Northwich, 35 ; 2, F Hardman, Huskisson, 35 ; 3, 0 B Walton, Chorlton, 40.</p>
        <p>SO Yards Egg and Spoon Race.-1, T P Miller, Cook and Sons ; 2. L Huton. Manchester; 3, T Burgess. Hartford. Half. Mile Haudicap Flat Race.-1, J Craddock, Warring- ton, 50 ; 2, A Birtwhistle, Northwich, 25 ; 3, W B Richmond, Victoria-street, 40.</p>
        <p>60) Yards Obstacle Race.— 1, J Roberts. Walton; 2, J Mason, Hnskisson ; 3. G Blaokshaw, Northwich._</p>
        <p>80 Yards Haadicap Sack Race.-1, J Windsor, Brunswick, G. 8.. 2 yards; 2, W H Welsh, Brunswick, 7; 3, 3 If Forster, Warrington, 10. _ _</p>
        <p>One Mlle Handicap Flat Race.-1, J M'Leod. Warrington, 135; 2, W B Richmond, Victoria-street, Mid., 85 ;3, F Speed. Birkenhead. Consolation R,ace.-1, H Birtwhiatle ; 2. W Hughes; 3, P Gillett. The prizes were delivered at the close of the competi- tions by Mr. D. Meldrum, who congratulated those present on the beautiful afternoon they had enjoyed, and also made some very pertinent remarks as to the value of such gatherings in promoting good feeling betvrees the various branches of the company's service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>QUOITS.</head>
        <p>QUOITS.</p>
        <p>ROPE AND ANCHOR (WOOLSTON) v. BLACKBE&amp;R. LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Played at Woolston on Saturday, ending in a victory for the homa team by 25. Score :</p>
        <p>ROPE AND ANCHOR, BLACK BEAR. Wm Stafford .. 13 T Nixon John Brown l3 F Daniel's Peter Walsh H Gatley J Monks Wm Clarke</p>
        <p>H Daintith __ __ _ - -</p>
        <p>J. Stafford l3 H Tailor ll Wm Lythgea</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T., No. 1. CONVENTION, S.W. LANCASHIRE. The monthly session of the above was held on Saturday, in the Central Hall, George-street, St. Helens. Bro. Vint, C. Counsellor (Ashton), presided over an excellent attendance of representatives from Warrington, Ashton, St. Helens, Earlestown, Sutton, Haydock, Widnes and Atherton. After the appointment of various committees, Bro. Newman, C. Sec. (Earlestown), read his monthly report, which showed that good work was being done throughout the Convention area. The report was adopted after some discussion. It was decided to hold the half-yearly session at Ashton- in-Makerfield, on Saturday, August 12th. Some very good reports were given by the representatives present from the lodges. Bro Reid, juvenile council treasurer (Liverpool) gave a very able address, urging the members to more active work. Sister Sherrington (Haydock), read an ex- cellent paper on " Drink, and its intluen3e.” After referring to the influence drink has upon the nation Sister Sherrington regretted that drunkenness was still on the increase among the women of the land. A vote of thanks was passed to Sister Sherrington. Bro Gwilym, PGCT (Liverpool), gave a very in- teresting address on the good of the order. A vote of thanks was passed to the St. Helens members for the arrangements made for the session. A vote of condolence was ordered to be sent to Bro and Siscer O'Brien (Warrington) on the dtath of their lather.</p>
      </div>
