      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MESSRS. PETER WALKER AND SON'S ANNUAL</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PETER WALKER AND SON'S ANNUAL</p>
        <p>EXCURSiON.</p>
        <p>The annual excursion oi the firm of Messrs. Pcter Walker and Sou to,k place on Saturday, the staff,work- people and friends numbering about 800. To the invi- tations sent out apologies were received from the f- lowing : rhe \favor (Alderman J. E. Wright). Alder- man Sutton, anl Messrs. R. Wood, T. Gibson, Captain Hutchinson, Mr. James Spurr (Blackpool), and others. The men hid been most generously provided by their employers with railway tickets which also passed them from Blackpool to Douglas and back by steamer and gained them free admission to the Blackpool Tower and entertainments. The arrangements had been admirably carried out by Mr. James Munro, Mr. F. Fulton Smith, Mr. A. Potter, and Mr. Councillor Baird and it is granted on all sides that this year the trip was more successful than any previous one. The train left Bank Quay at 6.17 and arrived at Blackpool at ten minutes to eight and Mr. Longto;ttom, the railway company's representative was complimented for the quick run. After breakfast the majority of the excursionists availed themselves of their prvilege ad embarked on the" Queen of the North for Douglas. Sorne.however, thinking the weather unpro- mising s-ayed in Blackpool. The weather changed con- siderably and the pessage was made under delightful conditions. Douglas was reached about two o'clock and after two hours' pleasant stay on the Island the parey comeneec-id their return journey, on a calm sea with a pleasant breeze. Tea was served to the staff and guests in the saloon and this thoughtfulness on the part of the firm was appreciated. On re-arrival at Blackpool the excursionists separated to enjoy the time at their disposal and experience some of the pleasures the others had been having during their absence. The organization of the huge scheme was excellent in every respec% and the whole arrangements were carried thorough expeditiously and smoothly. The chief credit for this was due to Mr. J. Fulton Smith. Mr. James Monro accompanied the excursion, and was highly grati- fied with its success. Upon their arrival at Blackpool in the evening, the stiff and guests were entertained to an excellent dinner at the County and Lane Ends Hotel, and at its c inclusion the usual toist list was gone through. Mr. J. Monro presided, end the vice-chair was occupied by Mr. J. Fulton Smith.</p>
        <p>Toe CHURMAN, in proposing the toast of "The Queen" said that seven years after their beloved Queen as- cended the throne a little brewery was started in War- rington, a town of then abut 10,000 inhabitants. Since that time Warrington had progressed consistently with the development of the Empire until it was now a large town with many enormms businesses, some of which indeed were to be numbered among the largest and most sucensful in England. (Hear, hear.) As War- rington had developed that little brewery business had become a lo'g one, and his hope was that both the town and business would go on and prosper and that their re- putation stand for only a tenth part of the time the revere -I memory of the Queen would last. (Aloplause.) The toast having been enthusiastically drunk, the toast of " The Prince and Princess of Wales and the rest of the Ryal Family" was honoured. The CHAIRMAN proposed the toast of" The Army, Navy and V thmteers," and said it was their hope that they would only be called out to advance civilization and to assist the poor and helpless. (Applause.) The toast was duly honoured.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooks sang " The fig that braved a thousand years."</p>
        <p>Mr. BRAIDE proposed " Th, Town and Trade of War- rington "and in doing so coupled with the toast the name of Councillor Lee.</p>
        <p>Song by the Chairman. "Death of Nelson." Councillor LEE said that be supposed his name had been coupled with the toast because he happened to be a snember of the Corporation. The Corporation of War- rington' they were aware, had just promoted a Bill which they hopod, and he firmly believed, would en- courage and promote the trade of the town, for by it powers had been acqaired which in the future would prove to be of the utmost usefulness. Messrs. Peter The development of :the business of: Messrs. Peter Walker and Son showed:how Warrington had progressed since ao it was initiated. (Applause.) Mr. W.H.J. JENKINS said it was his privilege and plea- sure to propose the toast of "Sacctss to Messrs. Pater Walklr and Son, Limited," coupling with it the name of Mr. Fulton Smith. (Applause.) It had occurred to him that there were two great things which tendered to the success of a commercial enterprise—on the part of Vats employer, courage and initiative with its attendant risks to capital and in other ways, and on the part of the employe earnest endeavour and a constant study of his employer's interests. (Applause.) He ventured to think that a blending of those qualities and their constant exercise had been productive and in no small degree of the success of the business of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son: (Applause).</p>
        <p>The tna,t was drunk with the greatest enthusiasm, and Mr. J. Fultpn Smith on rising to respond was n- ceived with the singing of " He's a jolly good fellow."</p>
        <p>Mr. FULTON SmITH returned thanks for the cordial manner in wnich they had honoured the toast of the firm. A:lopos of the Chairman's song, he could add that their success was also owing to the lac': that every man did his duty. (Applause). The toast of" The Press " was honoured, on the pro- position of Councillor J. BAIRD.</p>
        <p>_ - Mr. BENSON (Burton-an-Trent), proposed the toast of "The Railway Companies," and it having been duly honoured.</p>
        <p>Messrs. THOMPSON (MidIand),LONGBOTTOM (London and North-Western), and BICKFORD (Great Northern), res- ponded. The Great Central Company was represented by Mr. Gibbens, the Great Western by Mr. Taylor, and other representatives of the London and North-Western were Mes.rs. Cotton and Marshall. The CHAIRMAN said they bad two great powers to thaßk for the success of their excursion. The first was nature, which had favoured them with beautiful weather, and the second was Mr. Fulton 'Smith. (Ap- plause.) N)thin; had interfered with their enjoyment, 1 and be bad very great pleasure in asking them to cor- dially thank Mr. Smith for the admirable way in which he had organised the excursion. (Loud applause.) Song. Mr. Benson," Dear old Pals." Mr. FULTON SMITH thanked the comnany very heartily for their r( coption of the Chairman's kind expressions. Mr. Benin, in proposing the toast of "The Chairman," said he had collie amongst them only very recently. They were all pleased that be had accompanied them on their excursion, and he asked them to drink to his success and prosperity. (Applause.) The toast was drunk with musical honeurs. The CHAIRMAN thanked them very much for respond- ing so heartily to the appreciative words of Mr. Baird. Such geniality and kindliness as he had found at War- rinnton had agreeably surprised him. (Applause.) The excursionists left by special train from the Cen- tral Station a few minutes before eleven, and arrived safely at Warrington two hours later, the band playing a parting selection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>BICYCLE AccmE NT.</p>
        <p>Charles Silver, 160, Evelyn-street, was knocked down by a cyclist on Monday morning, and sus- tained a fracture of the ankle. He was taken to the Infirmary, where his injury was attended to. CYCLhS,—Special Ladies and aents Machines at in 15s. See Windows, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge-st., Warrington. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL, UNDER THE CLOCK. —2ldvt KICKED BY A HORSE.</p>
        <p>Thomas Garvin, 35, Bing-street, was bringing a horse for Mr, Connor from the Central Station on Tuesday evening, when the animal kicked him and sprained his ankle. Be was taken to the Infirmary WORKING MEN'S BOOTS, Best in Warrington at SCALES &amp; SONS, 83, Bridge-street. Warrington. SPIRITUALISM.</p>
        <p>On Sunday Mr. J. Kay delivered two addresses subjects, "Is evil terminable ?" and "After deith, what ?" and in each case followed with clatrvoy- ance. At the evening meeting Lily Gallop, a lyceu- mist, gave a recitation entitled "The Promised Land." On Monday evening Mrs. Williams, of Manchester, paid her first visit to this society, and gave a large number of clairvoyant delineations and psychic readings. BE sure and TRY ONE of J. BAYLEY "ISAAC'S PORK PIES. They are a luxury. —Advt. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH TEACHERS' TRIP TO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CHESTER.</head>
        <p>CHESTER.</p>
        <p>A number of the Holy Trinity Church Sunday school teachers went for their annual picnic on Saturday to Chester. They were accompanied by the Vicar (the Rev. E. C. E. Carleten) and Mrs. Carleton, the Rev. T. W. Barber, the school officers and a few friends to the number 31together of about 40. The party went down the river to Eccleston Ferry, where they partook of light refreshments, after which they paid a visit to the grounds of Eaton Hall, returning to Chester by conveyances for a good tea at the Dee Cafe. The excursion proved to be a most enjoyable one. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS —A.dvt SECOND HAND CYCLES. LARGE QUANTITY for SALE or HIRE,---J, S. GARLAND. Bridge-street, War- rington. WARRINGTON BOYS' SUMMER CAMP.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon about 70 poor Warrington boys went by the four o'clock train from the Cen- tral station to Birkdale, near Southport, for their innual week's holiday under canvas. The arrange- ments were made by the authorities of the Boys' ttefuge, Strangeways, Manchester, and through the exerlions of Mr. Arthur Lee subscriptions have been collected to pay the expenses. The Warrington con- inant-was selected by Mr Jones, scho)l attendance ffic4r,Und they will ha undar the cm:amend of a orporal and a sergeant. Next Monday a second atchof boys will be despatchei, and later on the oorgirls will have their outing.</p>
      </div>
