      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>MARRIkGE OF LORD DELAMERE. PICtURESQUE CERENIONY AT KNIGIIV- BRIDUE.</p>
        <p>lathe presence of a—large and distinguished con- gregation a very interesting and picturesque wedding took place on Tuesday afternoon at half- past two o'clock at St. Paul's, Knightsbridge. The bri -legroom was Lord Delamere,of Vale Royal,North- wich, Cheshire, while the bride was Lady Florence Cole, youngest daughter of the Earl and Countess of Enniskillen. The church was most artistically decorated with palms and white flowers, and the service was fully choral. Canon Cholmondeley Per- formed the marriage ceremony, assisted by the Rev H. Villiers. The Earl of Ennis- killen gave his daughter away. The bride's beauti- ful wedding gown was composed of white crêpe de chine, gracefully draped with flounces of Brasses lace. The long transparent train was made of the same exquisite lace. The bride's tulle veil was arranged over a coronet of orange blossom,and she wore no jewellery of any kind. Mr Seymour was the best man. Lady Kathleen Cole, Lady Evelyn Crichton, Lady Beatrice Taylour, and Miss Freda Villiers acted as bridesmaids. They wore very pretty dresses of white mousseline de soie, daintily arranged with fichus of the same, while their hats were of white straw, trimmed with white feathers and La France roses. Their bouquets were also of La France rases, and they were diamond shamrock brooches, the gifts of the bridgroom. The bride was attended by three little pages, in white satin cavelier co-tumes, the small twin sons of Sir Philip and Lady Grey Egtrton, and Master Ashley, cousin of the bride. After the ceremony a reception was held at No. H. Cadogan-square, and later in the afternoon Lord and Lady Delamere left for Broad lands Park, Hampshire, where the honeymoon will be spent. The bride's travelling dress was of blue mousseline de gaze, worn with a straw hat,trimmed with La France roses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUDGE-GARTL.kND.</head>
        <p>RUDGE-GARTL.kND.</p>
        <p>The wedding took place on Mu lay afternoon at the Church of the Sacred Heart, Bank Quay, of Mr. Edward Rage, Stockton Heath, and Miss Daisy Agnes Gartland, of Sankey-street. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. Father Ryan, and there was a large attendance at the church. The bride was dressed in cream cashmere and white silk gimp. She carried a bouquet of white roses and lilies of the valley, the gift of the bridegroom. She also wore a hat to match. She was acenmpanied by two bridesmails, the Misses M Gartland and E Rudge, They wore grey costumes trimmed with pink silk, and hats to match. They carried bouquets of pink roses, the gifts of the bridegroom. The groomsmen were Messrs. W. H. Radge and J. Tom- kit's. Several selections of music.: were played on the organ by Miss Bellian, and after the ceremony Afendelssohn's " Wedding March" was given. The Wedding breakfast was served at the bride's home, when a large number of guests were present. The happy couple left in the evening for Blackpool, where the honeymoon will be spent. A large num- ber of valuable and useful presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>CL kRKE-BROWN.</head>
        <p>CL kRKE-BROWN.</p>
        <p>A large number of persons assembled on Tuesday at St. Paul's Church to witness the marriage of Mr. John Clarke, a stocktaker in the service of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. and Miss Caroline Mary Brown, daughter of Mr. George Brown, of Orford- lane, and head mistress of the infants' department of St. Paul's day schools. The brills wore a pretty dress of white silk merveilleux trimmed with chiffon and " true lover's knot" and silk passemen- terie, train, and wreath and veil. She carried a beautiful shower bouquet and a white Prayer Book, the gift of the bridegroom. Her bridesmaids Mies Brown and Miss Maud Brown, wore yellow fancy silk dresses trimmed with chiff.m, and chiffon sashes and hats to match. Each also carried a white Prayer Book presented by the bridegroom. The best man was Mrs. George Brown' jun, of Liver- pool (brother of the bride), and the officiating clergymen the Rev. H. V. Pigot (late vicar of Sc. Paul's) and the Rev. E. R. Tarbuck, the present vicar. Mr. Ernest Wilkes was at the organ and supplied appropriate music. At the conclusion of the ceremony the wedding guests dP)ve to the in- fant's schools in Kendrick-street for breakfast, which was supplied by Mr. William Chantry, and before separating Mr. George Brown, jun., propased the health of the bride and bridegroom, Mr. Clarke suitably returning thanks on behalf of himself and his bride. The officiating clergymen, both of whom were present, were also thanked for their services, the Rev. E. R. Tarbuck referring in eulogistic terms to the valuable services rendered to the day schools by the bride, and also to Mr. Clark's work in the parish. Later on the happy couple lett for a tour in North Wales. The carriages were supplied by Mr. H. F. Greenough.</p>
        <p>LAWN MNISTIS :--F:LAr PARK V LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Played at Latchford, on Saturday, and resulted in favour of Elm Park by 17 events to 4, 35 sets to 9. and 243 games to 148. Latchford had several of their best players absent. A P Thomas (E P.) beat Jil Fisher (L) 6-3 6-1, J ( E.P. ) beat R Fairclough (L) 6-1 6-1, 0 H Oliver (E.P.) beat Rev J H Cameron (L) 6-1 6.2, W R Neilson (Er), beat D Fairclough (L), 6-4 7-5, F Bradford (E.P.), beat A J Sherwood (L) 6-1 6-3, R Gillison (E P.), beat L Fairclough (L) 6-4 1-6 6 3 T H Bell (E.P.), beat H Fairclough (L), 6-0 6-0, J Richardson (E P.) beat B Fairclough (L) 6-2 6-0, Thomas and Gillison beat Fisher and R Fairclough 64 64, Oliver and Neilson beat Cameron and D Fairclough 6-3 6-2, Bradford and Gillison beat Sherwood and L Fairclough 6-1 6-1, Bell and Richardson beat H and B Fair- Fairclough 6-0 6-3. Mrs. Riley and Miss Macphail (E.P.), beat Miss Dutton and Miss Fairclough (L) 6-2 7-5, Miss M Macphail and Miss Gillison (E.P.) beat Miss Roberts and Miss M Fairclough (L) 6 4 6-4, Miss Gracie and Miss Dwerryhouse (E.P.) lost to Miss J Fairclough and Miss Chandley (L) 4 6 4-6, Mrs Riley and J Gillison lost to Miss Dut- ton and Fisher 3-6 1-6, Miss Macphail and Oliver beat Miss N Fairclough and R Fairclough 6-4 6-4 Miss M Macphail and Neilson lost to Miss Roberts and Rev J Cameron 1-6 7-5 5-7, Miss Gillisoa and Bradford lost to Miss N Fairclough and D Fairolough. 1-6 8-10, Miss Gracie and Gillison beat Miss J Fairclough and Sherwood 63 6-1, Miss Dwerryhourio and Bell beat Miss Chandley and L Fairclough 8 6 6-1. _ _ _</p>
        <p>OThe proprietors of the Royal Oak Soap Works tender their apologies to their patrons for any inconvenience that may have been felt ia getting their Coupons for free Tablets of Britannia Herb Soap exchanged. This has been due to the excessive call for these Tablets and the makers beg to Intimate that they have now very considerably increased their plant in this department to cope with the growing demand so that n,o further delay *ill be experienee3. They ake this opporttruty of mentioning that they have now introduced a best Pink Carbolic Soap under the brand "Giant Oak" with which the free tablets of Herb Soap are given same as with the Royal Oak. Everybody should use hese high class soaps,— Adet ,</p>
