      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EXAMINEE,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>JULY 14th 11399.</p>
        <p>DOUBTLESS the supporters of Le gislation by the Government are in a Closure. ridiculous position over the Titbc A Bill. They do not like it, and they decline to do, more thawvote font. They know it is un- P°Pi liar, and they suspectthat it will damage the Go% rernment in the country and imperil their own sea ts. But they have not the manliness to ex- PrE. iss their dislike, or to warn the Government, or to xeiuse tosupport it. Their silence is held, how- " oar,to'be an excuse for other things,and amongst 04,ers for rulings from the Chair which are re- varkablo and lead to singular situations. We have tiheiChairman of Committees declining to allow discussion because the matters mentioned will 4aore properly arise on some subsequent amend- rtaeut, which may be declared to be out of order or tut of place, or which may be swept off by the 'Closure. ;He has also assumed that it is for him -to determine whether an amendment is of a slight •orof a substantial character, and to govern his .eonduct in the matter of imposing the closure by theludgment he makes. Ho might just as well 'deliver himself of a judgment at once, and de- "dare that half-a-dozen substantial amend- ments are worth discussing, and the intervals between them may be altogether cleared by means Of the closure. We do not desire to lay any stress O n an apparent willingness to allow Mr. LONG and the few speakers on the Ministerial side to be discursive, whilst speedily arresting discursiveness on the ,part of members of the Opposition. Upon this point the country will form its own opinion, •en the evidence of the Parliamentary reports, and it is,not,very likely that the verdict will be agree- able to Mr. LOWTEER, who seems to have decided fromthe first beginning of the Committee to give the ,Government every possible assistance in his Power. That may be his duty, but hitherto it has been the custom to veil it more decently. The free use of the closure,however, becomes the sequel -to the,policy of silence imposed on the Ministerial- jets, first by their own views of the Tithes till, and then by the organising officials of the Ministry. The result is not government by discussion,which is the only basis of our system, but legislation by the closure. Oa the whole, Perhaps, we ought not to object, except in the in- terests of Parliamentary decency and fair play. The dole to the clergy is better to be carried by the closure than in any other way. It is the l'ltEltiEn's policy, forced upon a non-enthusiastic Government, and the Leader of the House accord- ingly forces it forward by the gag. For purely Party objects, nothing more effective could be desired, The exhibition will be of immense ser- l'foe because it demonstrates the unreasoning liature of the support given to the bill, and im- Po—ssibility of defending it except by the rambling nonsense conveyed in the wordy speeches of the Minister of Agriculture, who is an ardent believer la the muzzle and the general process of stamping out. What the Liberal Unionist Nonconformists !tay think of this policy it would be interesting to gnow. They assisted in disestablishing the Irish Church, and now they are assisting in further en- dowing the English Church. If they have any Liberalism left, it ought to show itself quickly.</p>
        <p>AT the present moment Colonial Policy and Aid, there are some indications of great interest to sane and moderate Imperialists. The Cape Cabinet, by its leading members, under the guise of co-operation, seems to be attempting something outside its Province. It cannot be contended that it is any Part of the duty of Messrs. 110FIKEYR and CIIREINER to determine what shall and shall not begraperial policy. They will probably resent this description of the objects of their intervention between the Imperial Government and the Trans- They will say that they have endeavoured to modify a dangerous situation, so far as their f!lebds, the Boers, are concerned. That is true, as as it goes, but it is hardly the whole truth. 'lleY have not insisted upon the acceptance of '47hat the Imperial Government required. If .110. Y had a duty in the matter, that was Its, exact scope. Everything else was out- de their province. They were not invited °Y the High Commissioner or the Colonial Office t°Anterpose. It was a purely voluntary and 4.4kieable matter they undertook; and neither of eta has any official or moral right to say that the 'esults of the mission are adequate and ought to be 4eceptecl. This is to presume to dictate what the 13°110Y .of the Imperial Government ought to be alici this is just what the circumstances do not 14311" g• The Imperial policy must be determined . Imperial officials in the interests of the Empire, tat as the Transvaal policy has been influenced Ylike consi&amp;rations. We make no pronounce- On the concessions obtained. They may be red or bad, but they are not the terms put forth Six ALFRED M,ILICER. The Australian Colonies of °W 804th Wales and Queensland take a much a</p>
        <p>more sensible view of the position. They have proffered military aid to the Imperial Government in case it should be compelled to proceed to ex- tremities. The former has offered to equip and maintain two hundred and fifty mounted men, who are accustomed to the tactics of the Boers in fighting. The latter has placed at the disposal of the Government the Lancers now in England. The spirit of patriotism which animates Australia is to be warmly commended. There is the true kind of sympathy and the true kind of support. The Government of Cape Colony has more sympathy with the Boers that it has with the Outlanders; and so it has, by its leaders endeavoured to arrange a basis of settlement which is favourable to the Boers—more favourable, ap- parently, than Sir ALFRED MILNER'S plan. The home Government declined external arbitration, and it did not invite the arbitration of the Govern- ment of the Cape. New South Wales and I'Queensland do not say: "You ought to go to war," but Cape Colony does say: "You ought not to insist upon anything more than we have induced PRESIDENT KRUGER to accept." The difference is very considerable. Had the Cape Government been truly loyal and Imperialist, it would have:backed up Sir A. MILNER'S terms,and that would have been the way to ensure their acceptance. Instead of doing that the policy has been to cut them down, and to pronounce ajudg_ ment on their sufficiency, in order to prejudge the situation and hamper the Imperial Government. We cannot help regarding this kind of interven- tion as injudicious, and as rather exasperating.</p>
        <p>Captain Watkins, the master of the American liner Paris, has had his certificate suspended for two years. In his report he candidly admits that the disaster was owing to an unaccountable error on his part, and as we are all liable to make mis- takes, we cannot help but feel some sympathy for him in his punishment, just tholgh it is. Mr. Watkins was an old and respected commander, and in the first instance it must have been a great grief to him to lose his splendid ship on the rocks of the English channel. The suspension of his license as master of: ocean steamers takes away his livelihood for two years at least, and probably ' spells professional ruin. He is to be commiserated on the misfortune which has befallen him, but which, no doubt, is somewhat lightened by the fact that ho is responsible for no loss of life. Together with that of Captain Watkins' sentence, comes the news that the Portia, bound from New York to Halifax has been wrecked on an island ten miles out of her course, her officers being apparently ignorant of their whereabouts. The Stella, Mohegan, and Drummond Castle disasters are not yet forgotten, and cause one to wonder whether our merchant sailors are losing their seamanship. It is hard to account for such catastrophes,though they may follow so simple a cause as an error in judgment, for of a certainty they did not happen so in the past. There is, unfortunately, no remedy. They that "go down to the sea in ships" are per- force obliged to place implicit trust in the com- manders of the vessels or not travel at all.</p>
        <p>There have been many explanations of the en- ormous trade prosperity achieved by the United States outside her own dominions, but we are only gradually arriving at what appear to be the true facts of the matter. It seems to be a fact, for instance, thatin many striking cases British firms could have had the contracts, but ,business was so brisk and they were so full of work that they could not entertain them. Another new fact comes from Russia. Russia has been giving American manufacturers very extensive orders for locomo- tives and for steel rails. Experience shews, how- ever, that American rails, while handsomely finished, are not by any means so durable as those of British manufacture, and in a very few years the Russian railway system will have to undergo expensive repairs. The locomotives, also, are showy rather than durable, and the Russian Government in the course of years will think less and less of them as the bill for repairs increases in magnitude. Germany has got together a very large volume of trade with her cheap goods. "'Made in Germany" usually means something that will fall to pieces in a few days, or years, as the case may be. It is a pity that America should have yielded to the temp- tation to follow in Germany's steps, for there is more than enough spuriousness in the world al- ready, an public morality is becoming seriously affected.</p>
        <p>It has long been obvious that there .would be plenty of work for any Board of Conciliation which the General Medical Council might choose to es- tablish for the benefit of friendly societies and their medical officers. Such a Board is about to be appointed, and as its work will be of very great importance to a large class of poor people, it be- hoves the friendly societies anti their medical offi- cers alike to tread cautiously. The drug question is of supreme importance. We fear that to go to the very root of the question, it must be admitted that the grievance would disappear if the societies would be less niggardly in payments. Certainly, the poor patients who need expensive drugs would benefit. A medical man cannot afford to recommend costly medicines which ho has to nay for out of his own pocket. The high price of anti-toxin, for instance, forbids its use in cases where the rate of pay received by the medi- cal man is only about .a halfpenny a week. He is, therefore, driven to disparage anti-toxin just as he would have to use less quinine or cod liver oil, or even avoid those commodities altogether, in case they rose in price. At one time the cost of quinine was so high that it had to be sold to friendly so- ciety patients at special rates. There are. some patent preparations at this very moment which would be recommended in suitable cases by the medical officers of friendly societies if they could afford to do so. It is objected by some friendly societies that if medical men cannot afford to keep the spirit of these cheap contracts wherein they undertake to supply "proper and sufficient medi- cine," they ought not to accept them. But, of course, like any other business man, a friendly society doctor accepts the contract with the inten- tion of cutting his garment according to his cloth. If the friendly societies desire to secure the best medical supervision for their members, they must insist upon fair payments.</p>
        <p>The Government has received some very hard knocks this session, but the result of the f )Idham election is the most crushing blow of all. From our point of view it is magnificent; and there is no semblance of a fluke about the way in which a large and important provincial town like Old- ham has returned two out-and-out Liberals in place of the two Conservatives who were elected in 1895. Oldham has deliberately, and of a set purpose, joined hands with Southport, Edinburgh and Stockport, and why ? Because—and there is no gainsaying it—the electors there, as in nearly every borough in the country, are sick unto death of the policy of the Government. The raids on the boroughs for the benefit of the squire and the parson are neither forgotten nor forgiven. A better opportunity of testing the feeling of a working-man community on some of the Government's recent measures could not have been wished for, and now that that opinion is pronounced the Government I has got its deserts and had better begin to think out some dignified way of retiring from office. But have they the courage to appeal to the country? No, they havenot. They will cling to their post —netlike the soldier in the face of stern duty, but like the man, who, drowning, clutches at a straw. It is worth while considering another aspect of the Oldham election. We had the strange sight pre- sented to us of a scion of the aristocracy running in harness with a Labour leader who holds Socialistic views, and is yet a Conservative. On the face of it such a combina- tion looked a strong card for the Conservatives to play but the Oldham folk are shrewd and would have none of it. A union of Toryism and Labour is a monstrosity and like other monstrosities it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY JULY 14, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY JULY 14, 1899.</p>
        <p>has bec-n accorded but a short shrift. The Oldham electors have plainly had more than enough of modern Toryism, and we are convinced that when the time comes the country will pronounce a similar verdict on the policy of doles and the general misgovernment of the party in office.</p>
        <p>THEFTS AT RUNTJORN,-A THIEF'S MODE OF ENJOYMENT.</p>
        <p>A COLOURED SAILOR SENT TO PRISON. A SPREE AT THE "ROOKERY," At the Runcorn Police Court on Friday moraine, before Messrs. F. J. Norman and F. Wiswall, considerable interest was evinced in the trial of a coloured man named James Brown, who obtained £2 by means of a trick; and Samuel Ellis. of Stringer's Court,Heath-road, Runcorn, who stole £ll from the flat " Fanny " whilst that boat was lying at the Ruecorn Docks. James Brown, a coloured man,described as an seaman of the Andorinlaa was charged with fraudu- lently stealing a pocket book, £2 in money, and a sea- man's discharge. the property of Lucas Huizing, on Thursday July 6ch 1899.—Lucas Huizing, stated be was a native of Rotterdam, at present employed on the sail- lug vessel " Andorinha ", tie same vessel as that on which the prisoner was employed. The night before last he had , a pocket book containing two sovereigns, some Dutch coins and a German discharge note which he gave to his landlord (Mr. Taylor) to keep for him for the night and to give it him the next morn- ing. The prisoner Brown,was present at the time wbich would be about 11 o'clock at night. The following day he left work about 6 o'clock after having tea on board his vessel, and in consequence of what Mr. Taylor told him he went in search of Brown. Eventually he found him in ' a public house—he could not tell the name—and asked him for his pocket book. Brown denied that he had it at first, but afterwards said "It was in the pocket of a coat hanging in the house,"and muttered something about fetching it. Brown then ran away from witness. He identified the pocket book and seaman's discharge note as his.—James Taylor, of the Vine Hotel, Low- land's-road, corroborated the previous witness as to having the pocket book, and two sovereigns, and the seaman's discharge left in his custody. The following morning James Brown came to him and said" the other man had gone to Liverpool, and that he was to have the things." Witness gave him the articles and Brown paid for some drinks spending lid at the house. He changed a sovereign with him and his "Missus" gave Brown 19a id change.— James Fox, outfitter, High-street, deposed to Brown buy- ing articles of clothing to the value of 14s 61. lie ten- dered a sovereign anl received 53 61 change.—The Superintendent remarked that other evidence tended to show that Brown had hired a landau and been spending a" high time" at Halton Castle, and driving about.— The prisoner elected to be summarily dealt with, and pleaded guilty. Nothing was known against him before only that one shilling was found on his person when arrested.—Brown said that he had bought. the clothing with his own money, having roceived £1 19s 21 the day before when paid off.—The Chairman observed it was very ingenious for him to say so, but they considered he had bought the clothes with the proceeds of his theft, and they would have to he handed over as pert ef the stolen property recovered.—He would be sent to Bouts- ford, for six weeks with hard labour. A MATE ROBS HIS CAPTAIN. Sarneiel Ellis, of Stringer's Court. Heath-road,Runcorn was charged with having stolen £ll, the money of John Littler, captain of the flat "Fanny," whilst lying at Runcorn on the 3:d of July.—Superintendent D. Pearson observed in opening the case that it was rather com- plicated. It appeared that John Littler, the captain. placed 11 sovereigns on a ledge in the cabin of his flat on the 3rd inst. At two o'clock the same day he saw the money safe. He then went to knock about the docks until 5 or 6 o'clock, after which ,he came to town and returned to his fiat, but did not miss the money until the following morning. Ellis (the Prisoner) had been in the employ of Littler as mate, ani in consequence of hearing from his son (William Littler) that Ellis had been on board he flat at about 8.20 the night before information WiS given to the police.Follnw- ing up the-clue,Constable'Carrington and Superintendent Pearson went to the house of a man named Radler' in Heath-road, with whom the prisoner lived and there found Ellis and his son and the man Radley in a beastly state of intoxication. They conveyed them to the Police Station and the following day both the Ellisees were pro- ceeded,. against, the younger being discharged and the senior remanded to that present court. Thomas Radley would now prove that the prisoner gave him a sovereign. Mre. Radley.'hiatwife,would also prove that she found £2 17s. id. (which he, however, thought prisoner' had been robbed of) and Mrs. Bazley would prove that 21. 61. had been given her on two occasions. —John Littler, 43, Princess-street,!Runcorn,emester and owner of the flat " Fanny " corroborated the statement made by Superintendent Pearson. Prisoner was em- ployed by him as hand on the Fanny and had been with him for about six weeks. He placed the 11. sovereigns oe the ledge of the bunker where the clean clothes ere generally placed. He did not know that prisoner knew of the whereabouts of the money, but as , mate be would have access to the cabin at all times though his place was on the other anteing I be had no business to go there. He gave information of bis loss to the police about ten o'clock the following morninse—By Superintendent Pearson : He usually kept his money , there.—By the Magistrates' Clerk: Prisoner's place was on the port side of the cabin and he had no business to go to the other side.—Thomas Radlev, labourer. Heath- road, stated he waa in the Egerton Arms, on ruesday morning in company with two or three other men. Whilst there Samuel Ellis came in. He asked witness to have a drink and he had a glass of whisky which prisoner paid for. Re (witness) was drunk on Monday night and awoke about 5 o'clock the following morning. He saw Ellis then as he was in the house lying on the sofa in the kitchen. He pulled a sovereign out of his pocket and said " Will you fellows have a drink ?" Witness replied yes." and Ellis gave him the sovereign, saving he had got the money on a berme "Flving?o."_Rebcea Baeley, wife of John Bazlev. of Stringer's Court, said she remembered Monday night laateshe went to the Ship nine o'clock in comnanv with llmnriscabnonadtleay. lltAttle the est " Shin " they saw Ellis, who treated them to a glass of whisky each. Ellis knocked her'gless over accidentally. Ellis said to her she could have 2s. 61. he had won on " Flying Fox." She took, it and paid for drinks, and took home grime beer for her husband. Afterwards she met Mrs. Radler and Ellis again ; they were eoing in the direction of the hotel Ellis said he would treat them if they would go with him. She replied that she would go and have the break- fast over, send her husband to work, and the children to 'sehool and then come. On reaching the inn she found Ellis treating the penole to drink. She had a elass of neppermint. Then Ellis gave her another 2s. 61. She po lei: ion g Station,sta statement ef nbt: wS Superinten- dent i annstaeE had returnrd the sg. to the police when she heard the money had been stolen.—Police-nonstable Carrington dent Pearson. After the prisoners were con- thtehe with Sergeant White searched be, preniises. corroborated o r at toe d They found the stocking cwoinffiened to her bed for over (produced) containing years. see was trying to conceal it. It contained ignevnaslidsalnid. wuhnodehrandeatbheetnhe 18 one sovereign and the rest in silver and copper. MTS. Radler had breught him £2 odd the following morning and said shelled "found it where the sticks were kept', He then saw Mrs. Bszley who admitted she had re- ceived two half crowns. She alto refunded the money. He charged the prisoner with etealing the money (£11.) from the flat " Fanny " on the previous Monday night, and he replied "I have nothing to say I plead guilty."—Jane Radley, wife of Thomas Radlev, gave further evidenee. Witness and went to Hughes shot, ayla got four pork pies. She re- prisoner left the Ship Inn together and Ellis membered John Littler coming in and shortly after they all went out —The Superintendent: Why did you all go out P—Witness : I wanted my man to go home.— Why did you take the prisoner home P—lle could not go alone—Someone assisted you to take him home P—A Mr. Rafferty. He was on one side and lon the other.— Wfiro you there when the policeman came P—l was not there and did not .es: the polineman.—What condition were the men in when he came P—l saw that Ellis and his son had had a deal of drink.—Dl von remember me questioning you rather closely. and is it not a fact tha t YOU took money out of his pocket P—No. sir. —Did YOU give your husband a sovereign ? No, sir—la the Egerton Arms did you net say to vonr husband Mr. B--you have been in my pockets P—No sir.--Then perhaps you will tell In how you found the money P—Witness then said she went into the pantry to get some sticks and saw a piece, of paper,and on undoing it she found the money.—Superin- tendent Pearson , Row did you account for the money being there P—Witness I don't know anything about the money.—Superintendent Pearson: You saw that the prisoner was very flush with money P—Witness : I did not know that a robbery had been committed. I found the two sovereigns, and I went out into the yard and told the neighbours, and they advised me to bring the money to the Police Station.—Prisoner in answer to the charge elected to be summarily dealt with. He could not recellect anythine about the affair, as be had had a power of drink. The first time he saw the money was whilat he was cleaning the deck ;a sovereign had fallen, and he discovered there were others there. He put it back then. On the Monday he had some drink, a gone and fetched e lot away. He was horn minßt have th Runcorn and oasiseci his 66'h year in age.— S uperintendent Pearson stated that hitherto the men had borne a good character. He asked also that no expenses he allowed two of the witnesses, Mrs. B lzley and Mrs. nesnev.—The Beneh sentenced prisoner to three months imprisonment without hard labour: BOOTS, CASH CIIHMISTS.—Letters of allotment and regret;have been posted in respect to the iSSIIe of Prefer- ence shares of Boot. Cash Chemists (Lancashire), Limited, which hal been very largely over subscribed. When asking for Cocoa, insist on haying Ctostrays--sold nly in Packets and Tins as other Cocoas are often suhsti- utsd for the sake of extra protit,.--Advt (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SRiMEN.</p>
        <p>A number of the seamen from the Runcorn Branch of this institution, had a most enjoyable ,outing on Overton Hills last Wednesday, through the kindness of one or two local patrons. The journey was made by brake. Football, cricket and bowls, were some of the amuse- ments indulged in • a capital tea was also provided and liberally done justice to, and though handicapped by slightly unfavourable weather, everyone returned to Runcorn, well sathfied with the outing.</p>
        <p>'AFTER MANY DAYS."</p>
        <p>Henry Lewson, late of Grindrod-square, was trought up in custody, at the Runcorn Police Court,on Thursday charged with neglectinz to answer a summons, issued on the 23rd March, 1896, charging him with the neglect of his four children in a manner likely to cause them suffering and injury to health.—Mr. W. Timmins who presided remanded prisoner until the following day.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK SCHOOL TREAT.</p>
        <p>The asnual treat of the -Br-u-nswick Sunday Schools took place on Wednesday afternoon last, and about 4bo availed themselves of the opportunity of a scramble over the Overton Hills. Tea was provided at Mersey View, before and after which games and amusements were indulged in. The weather proving somewhat un- settled drove the company under shelter at intervals, but diversification was found in dancing and music. The scholars were accompanied by their teachers and super- intendents, and also by the Rev. C. W. L. Christien (pastor). MILTON _P.S.A., WIDNES. .</p>
        <p>There was a very good attendance at the usual weekly gathering of this society on Sunday afternoon, when the President (Rev J Pedlar) occupied the chair. Misses Elwards and Hughes and Messrs. Baggeridge and Will. kinson sang a quartette, "0 Calvary," very effectively. and Miss Edwards sang very sweetly the solo, "Eternal Rest," to the evident appreciation of the members. The Rev. D Rogers, of Halstead, Essek,gave a telling address on "Prayer," showing that although there were prob- ably more prayers unanswered than answered, yet, he said, undoubtedly if we knew what was best for us there would be as much blessing bestowed by withholding the petition as in the granting of the same. The address was listened to with attention. A good meeting closed with the Benediction.</p>
        <p>METHODIST FREE CHURCH, WIDNES. The Sunday school anniversary seryics in connection with the Albert-read Church were held on Sunday, when the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. T. Sydney Morris morning and evening. There were good congre- gations. Ira the afternoon a children's service was pre- sided over by Mr. J. Whitehead, and an address was given by Mr. A. Poulson, which was listened to with delight. During the day, special hymns were sung by the children, and the choir rendered anthems in an able manner. On Monday a service of song entitled " Harry's Message." was given by the choir, Mr. J. S. Snoddy reading the story with effect, Mr. R. Wright occupied the chair. THREE MONTHS' IMPRISONMENT FOR BEGGING.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Tuesday, before Messrs. W. Timtnins and J. Ockleston,Williatn Woodier, a solderly looking young man, was charged under the Vagrancy Act with begging.—P.C. Bowyer proved the case and said the prisoner laad'carried on the practice for some time, gcing lath at night to houses and when he was refused he used abusive language. He had known prisoner for two years and never knew him do any work.—lnspector Hicks said he had known the prisoner for four years and he never knew him to do a days' work. He had had many complaints in regard to him. He was a corner loafer,and got his living by systematic begging. The prisoner had been no repeatedly for insulting females.—Superinten- dant Pearson said the prisoner was one of the worst characters in this part of Cheshire—The prisoner who had no defencc., was sentenced to three months hard labour, Mr. Timmins remarking that he had a bad record, and the Bench hoped this sentence would m tke a change in his life. LARCENY OF POTTED BEEF AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mary Ann O'Brien was brought up in custody at the Runcorn Police Court. on Wednesday morhing, charged with stealing a tin of roast beef and a tin of syrup valued at is 2d the goods of Samuel Crosby, grocer, Top Locks. Runcorn. The magistrates sitting were Messrs John Ookleston and F J Norman.—lt would appear that on Monday evening the prisoner got drunk and subse- quently fell into the hands of a constable who looked her up. The goods spoken about above were then found on hr person. Whilst in the cell she expreised a desire to interview a policeman and Constable Fry attended her. She then admitted having stolen the articles found upon her and if she was allowed to go out would show the shop she took them from. She was grant.d that request and Constable Fry accompanied her. The shop proved to be that of Mr. Crosby.—Mr. Arthur Crosby told the court he missed the articles on the Tuesday morning. He had similar goods in stock.--The prisoner who had been many times convicted at Warrington, and other places, was sen- tenced to two months' imprisonmant.—The charge of drunk and disorierly was then preferred against her.— Mr. Bowyer, school attendance officer, stated he saw the Prisoner selling lavender on the Monday. She called at his residence, and his wife refusing to boy any of her " wares." prisoner used a most offensive expression towards her. She was very drunk.—Prisoner was com- mitted for a month on this charge ; the sentences to run consecutively. CHAPTER OF ACCIDENTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>I On Friday, Ernest Worthington, of Wivern's Place, Runcorn, had a narrow escape of losing his life. He is employed as a painter by the London and North-Western Railway Company and on Friday was engaged on the Runcorn Bridge. He had been to the Runcorn Station and was on his was back welkin°, in the six-foot way. Seine; a down luggage train coming towards him he edged a little way near the up metals, and owing to the noise of the tranship failed to hear the 11.30 up express which was approaching from his rear. The result was that the buffer of the express caught him a severe blow on the elbow knocking him down so that he had a narrow escape as his head fell almost beneath the wheels of the other train. P.C. Thomas Bowyer and Mr. Trevite (confectioner) rendered "first aid" and Dr. Mc- Dougall being called attended the injured man who has escaped with but a slight fracture.—Messrs.Bowver and Trivett, however.had not finished their day's ambulance work for they were next called to attend George Smith in the empl yof Mr. George Duckett, who injured his head by a fall from the top of a shandry into the road- way. Smith was taking empty hampers to the Rail- way station at the time and was riding on top. As the vehicle ascended the incline to the station the baskets becoming detached caused Smith to be precipitated head formnet into the roadway. He was also attended by Dr. McDougall the extent of the damage being a severe scalp wound about two inches long.—Thomas Hall, conk of the schooner " Julia " of Gloucester. was making his way amongst some trucks which were being shunted at the docks when he got his arm severely pinched between the buffers of two of them. Mr. W. Shaw (MerAev Mission), rendered first aid and took the young fallow to Dr. McDougall's where his injures wore attended to gratis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FROD§-HAM.</head>
        <p>FROD§-HAM.</p>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY SERVICES —The 40th anniversary ser- vices of the Fire Croeses Sunday School were nducted in the chapel on Sunday by the Rev. R. J. Clifton. of Runcorn. Special hymns were rendered by the aurr- mented choir. Messrs. Joseph Berrington and S. Emmett, jun., blintz the conductor and accnmnanist. The offer- tories of the day realized £22 10L 5.</p>
        <p>CHURCH COUNCIL MEETING.--fit the quarterly meet- ing, of the Church Council the Vicar,who presided,stated that the fund fnr the new screen recently erected in the parish church amounted to 00 O. 6d., and he read a letter from Mr. Rodley, the architect, advocating the staining and polishing of it. After consideration it was thought advisable to leave the new screen as it is. The Chairman also proiucerl the brass plate, furnished by Mr. Bodies?, to be placed on one cf the piers of the west arch cloFe to the screen, and which hears the following inscrintion To the glory of Goi and in memory of John Robinson, M.R.C.S. and L.R.C.P.. seventeen years outer-warder of this church, who died April 3rd, 1894, in the elat year of his age. This screen was erected by his relatives and friends."</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN MAN IN TROUBLE.—John Crompton, a cooper, native of Runcorn, was fined 2?. 61. for being drunk in Bridge-lane. Froclsharn. at 10.30. p.m. on Sun- day night.—The case came before Mr. A. Thomas, on Monday. and was proved by P.C. Dickens. DISTRIBUTION or PRIZES.-0a Tuesday night the an- nual distribution of prizes took place at the Girls' S3hool, Frodsbam, and proved as happy and interesting a function as ever. The prizes were the gift of Miss Violet Hayes, of Frodsham, a lady well known for her interest and kindly patronage of all good works. and were distributed to the successful winners, about 120 in number by the Rev. H. B. Blogg (vicar). During the evening the girls performed many exquisite evolution drills with naive precision; and, also accomplished ex- ercises of a bewildering nature, the instruction into the mysteries of which must have given Miss Roberts (the head mistress) the expenditure of considerable time and patience.. SCHOOL TREATS AND OTHER Tan's.—The Free Church Council of Frodsham and district had a trip to Black- cool on Wednesday; the united choirs of the Frodaham Parish Church, and St. Dunstan's Church, visited Llan- dudno the same day ; and on Friday the annual trip of the Free Church Sunday schools, went to New Brighton Tower. From the accounts of the returned trippers, a very enjoyable day was spent all round.</p>
        <p>WIDOW WELCH'S PILLS FOR FEMALE COMPLAINTS SMITITERS, Grand-daughter to the Widow _L Welch, continues to prepare this celebrated Medicine from the only original and genuine Family Recipe. Its just celebrity for all Female Complaints has obtained for it a most extensive and increasing demand Purchasers should therefore attend to the following CAUTION,—To be careful that what they purchase is prepared by Mrs. Smithers, who Is the only Person in possess,on or the ORIGINAL RECIPE. The genuine are w ..apped in Blue Pc per, and signed on the Ou&amp;ide label by Mrs. Smithers, and the names of the Sole Wholesale Agents, " W. EDWARDS &amp; SON 157, Queen Victoria-street," formerly o! 67, St. Pau Is' London,are engraved on the Government Stamp, So:d in boxes Lt 24, 9d. each by most Chemist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. "SHALL WE GO BA.CK TO ROAM ?"</head>
        <p>BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. "SHALL WE GO BA.CK TO ROAM ?"</p>
        <p>The Brunswick Wesleyan Schools celebrated their 99th anniversary on Sunday and Monday last, in accordance with usual custom. Sermons were preached morning and evening at St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, by the Rev. E. Theodore Carrier. of Liverpool, supplemented with special music, efficiently rendered by the St. Paul's Choir, and scholars attending the schools. On the Mon- day evening, the Rev. J. T. Wardle Stafford delivered a lecture entitled " Shall we go back to Rome," in the form of an appal to Protestants, and under the presi- dency of Mr. James Dodds, of Lymm, an attentive audience listened earnestly to his eloquence. The con- gregations 012 Sunday were very large, the sacred edifice being packed at the evening service. The singinc, throughout was of a very high order, and reflects credit- ably upon the training both scholars and choir received at the hands of Messrs. John Birkett, and J. R. Pick- thall. The organist was Mr. R. Bowyer, whose accom- plished manipulation of the instrument, was highlyt appreciated. _ _</p>
        <p>-"hie Rev.E. Theodore Carrier's discourses were eloquent and masterly. In the morning he took for his text : " These sought their register among those that were reckoned by genealogy, but they were not found; there- fore, were they, as Donated, put from the priesthood." Ezra, 2. 62 v. He drew many beautiful inferences and cautioned his hearers against neglecting the examination of their genealogies lest they too should be put away ns polluted. At the evening service be founded an admir- able review of the book of Revelations upon the words : "And I beheld, and, 10, in the midst of the throne been slain." Revelation 53. 6v., and took his interested hearers with him as he graphically depicted the glories of the scene when the Limb, the Saviour of mankind, came forward to open the Book whose souls no one in Heaven or on earth was wGrthy to unloose save 1:le ; and when the whole universe burst into one harmonious smg "Worthy, is the Lamb." It was an act of three-fold adoration that those countless millions would take part in, mankind, the angels, and the whole animal creation took part in it. Beautiful was the picture the eloquent preacher painted in glow- ina language of the magnificent scene.</p>
        <p>In making the annotincernent respecting the lecture which was to be deliverei the following day by Mr. Wardle Stafford the preacher observed that the subject Mr. Stafford was going to discourse upon was of great importance to them and embraced the question "Shall we go back to Rome ?" He knew to that question they could but raturn one answer. What they were most afraid of was the house that stood half way towards it —their own National Church. Ir was the ritualist that had done so much to undermine the doctrine and prin- ciples of their own National Church. It behoved them therefore as those who loved the principles of the refor- mation to do all they could to put down any attempts to abuse them.</p>
        <p>The musical portion of the services deserves special mention and seldom if ever has the singing been so harmoniously blended and hearty at a Runcorn anniversary, the pieces" Be a Hero" given at the morning service, and the "King's Brigade " at the evening, being the most popular. The Jubilee hynem " 0 King of Kings" was also rendered by request. Per- haps the choir was best heard in the" Beautiful vale of rest "and the chant " The sun declines," both of which were capital performances. THE LECTURE. On Monday night there was a fair assemblage in St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, when the Rev. J. T. Wardle Stafford delivered an appeal to Protestants entitled "Shall we 2'o back to Rome ?" The chair was taken by Mr. James Dodds, Lymm, who was supported by the tollowing gentlemen in addition to the Lecturer :—Re vs. C. W. L. Christien (circuit superintendent), R J Clifton, W Fidoe, Messrs. A.R. Norman,J.P., F, J. Norman R.H. Poenett, W. Harrison )Livernool),J. W. Seeley, G. Bellamy, T Simister, S. Pope, J. Povah,and E. Marshall. The Chairman (Mr. James Dodds), who was cordially received, said he thanked them for their kind reception to him, an entire stranger. He was there in accordance with a promise made some time ago, and was prepared to do his utmost to fulfil that. Before saying anything to them about the subject which Mr. Wardle Ste.Zord would lay before them, he wished to compliment them upon the satisfactory audience assembled there, especially in face of the Other attrac- tions. It was a pleasure to feel that the friends of the school should come out in such numbers on such an occasion. He felt he ought not to inflict a speech upon them, as he came by train and had already delayed the meeting some ten minutes. He would only say that their Methodist Church was not tainted with this Romish heresy. John Wesley himself was as harmless a Protes- tant as any they had ever heard, and in these days there wag no Church snore intensely loyal to the principles— the great princinles—of the Reformation, than that of the Methodist Church. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The Rev. Wardle Stafford, who was enthuniastically received, said he took it that it was of vital import- ance to all professing: Christians that they were able to give a reason for the hops that was within them. He took it that they should not only have a conviction but they Rhould be able to reason that which underlay the conviction." "Why am I a Protestant ?" " Why am I a Nonconformist ?" were not questions they could smile out of court, and among other things they had to give an answer to those questions. He had prepared his lecture for educational rather than popular purposes and if his lecture was devoid of humorous anecdote and flippant epigram it was simply because he felt the gravity of the subject he had taken in hand. With this explanation the lecturer Proceeded to deal with his subject, and in the course of his very eloquent remarks said : At the outset he wished to say that he did not sympathise with the methods employed by Mr. Kensit and some of his friends, and did not think that anything was to be gained by brawling in church, nor did he think that persecution would exterminate those principles. He took it that the present crisis was in a great measure due to the Bishop and Arch-Bishop being unable to stand up firmly themselves. Referring to Mr. Walsh's book " The secret history of the Oxford movement," the speaker aaid it was a book that appealed to Evangelical churchmen, broad churchmen, and even high church- men of the type of the late Dean Wilberforce. The his- tory of that movement laid hare the history of the Ritua lists in its exposure of the Society of the Hely Cross, which was the most powerful of these eecret bodies. It began' with fits three members who joined the Church of Rome and were ordained ministers. Two of those ministers were now known but the third had not yet been discovered. He spoke of the literature issued by this organisation and of the memerable occa- sion upon which they were referred to in the Rouse of Lards when it was then stated that some of the publica- tions were so filthy,that they were kept out of the news- papers and were not even published in Hansard. For the benefit of his vnung hearers he explained the posi- tion the Pope held in the Catholic Church of Rome. They recognised him as the entire head of creation. He came before the King and their aim was to subject the civic Powers of all countries to the Church. He the Pone was infallible, especially when he spoke in hie official con- nection with the Cuurch. Now if that was so, if the Pope was infallible, should he not be infallible in his morals ? But that was far from the case and here the speaker instanced several Popes whose abominations he would blush to disclose' to the full. Dealing next with the organisations of the Ritualistic Party the rev.gentleman disclosed the sophistic methods these bodies adopted of furthering their aims. How secret they were in orteration would be illustrated when he said that one of these societies issued something like 15 thousand official papers weekly, but it was rarely that one of these organs got into the hands of a non- member. Dealing with the doctrine of Transubstantia- tion he quoted the Scripture: "Jesus took bread and bleresed it and brake it, and gave i to the disciples and said Take, eat, this is my body.' And he took the cup and gave thanks and cave it to them saying 'Drink ye all of it ; for this is my blood.' They were aware that the Romish Church taught that the literal signifi- cance should be attached to the words " this is my body" and "this is my blood." But he pointed out for the benefit of those Biblical students ' who might be present that it could not be so. For Christ was not then dead. He himself look the bread and wine and gave it them with his own hands. Again the Church of Rome had always insisted upon the supremacy of the Pope and that they founded upon the verse" Thou art Peter and upon this Rock will I build my Church," but again be would call the attention of the etuderit, and especially the young, to the fact that the original ran thus "Thou art petros (the original word for stone) and nom this petrae (original for rock) will I build my Church." • Who was the petros ; the stone; the portion of the original rock ; why Peter! But who It as petrae ? Why. manifestly. the Lard Jesus Christ him- self, and it was noon that rock that the Church wag to be built! Next speaking of the Homilies of Pope Clement, the speaker said that there was not an intelligent Catholic of the present day who would deny that they were forgeries. He asked therefore were they to look to a Church that was founded on fraud and deceit for guidance? A Church that nut itself befere the Bible. They were Metho- dists, and they were cradled in Methodism ; they loved Methodism, and some of them were even prepared to die for Methodism. But though they loved it as their very selves, they always put the Bible before Methodism (Applause.) Speaking of music, he said they (Meth . diets) loved a harmonious, service, but they did no.. believe in choir performances and intonations. !rs archnt tecture they loved taste, but theirs was a plain taste'-. The grand ceremoninl and narsphsnalia, the burning of incense and the elevation of the Cross were not concise dive to a holy worship. They were Puritans, and though they differed in some respects with the Puritans of old —inasmuch as they were more advanced —they still upheld the great principle of Puri- tanism. Dealing with the Jesuit Order, Mr. Stafford quoted from their principal writers several extracts which he semmed up as connivance at equivo- cation "They were first to convince themselves that</p>
        <p>they were justified in lying ; aol then:lio like troopers." That was the principle that underlay the whole of the extracts he submitted. Surely then those were not the people they should be in a hurry to go back to. They who bad been taught to look upon truth as something sacred, were they going te allow their country to go to men of that stamp? Already he began to sea the priestly mein and sallow cleric, walking the lanes and reads of their country places. Were they going to allow it? Speaking of Cardinal Newman, the lecturer observed that he had listened to Mr. Walsh (the author of the secret history of the Oxford movement), the other day at Birmingham, and hoard him call attention to a re- markable incident. Mr. Walsh pointed out that New- man in none of his writings makes the slighseit refer- ence to his ordination as a Roman Catholic Priest. Now it was well known that all ministers of all denominations thought a lot of their ordination; but none so much as the Roman Catholic Priest. Yet Newman never men- tions it. He. the lecturer, wished to say that he was not one of those who were prepared to say that Newman was an unmitigated scoundrel or anything of the sort; and he believed with certain reservations that Cardinal Newman was a great Saint. But to continue, Mr. ' Walsh said he had reason to believe that Newman was consecrated sometime in the year 1832. And he re- mained in the Church of England hint!l he was taken over to the cnurch of Rome with honour about 20 years after. No doubt Newman was able to do this on the principal of " It is not a lie to tell a falsehood if anyone asks about anything which he is not allowed te ask which was a quotation from one of the Jesuit Fathers. They had a duty to perform to protect England from the "priest." What did they see in the fate of France, Spain, Austria, and other continental countries which had been dominated by the priest? They had lost their greatness and grandeur and that would be the fate of England. Once they kr the priest step in between father and son, husband and wife, their domestic happi- ness would be at an end. Nonconformity had fought and won tremendous battles in this country. He spoke of the stirring times of Elward 111. and Ella Oath ; the advantage of the English Church, the departure of the puritan fathers from the shores of England in the me- morable year 0f11652,0f their nobleness and self-sacrifices for their principles,and of their heroic wives and daugh- ters. Those were days when they "persecuted and said all manner of evil against them for Christ's name sake." Those were the people he wished they would all follow. Why did their young men and maidens wear those sym- belie cresses,which though studded with precious stones theirr forefathers would have thrown into the sea ? Continuing, the lecturer in a thoroughly argu- mentative style refuted the Dogma and Doctrine of the Romish Church and finally exhorted his hearers to up- hold the worship of God in its purity. The lecture, which lasted about an hour and a quarter was inter- rupted with well merited applause and at its conclusion the .audience burst forth into spontaneous acclamation.</p>
        <p>The Chairman remarked that the lecturer had given a serious turn to their thoughts. Sill there was one point that he felt keenly upon and that was if the work was to be done at all they would have to first of all see that the Church was detached from the State. Why was the Church to have their support if they were go- ing to indulge in those abominations ? When that was accomplished he thought that the Methodist Church would be able to take up its position in the very fore- front of the free churches of their country. He exhorted them all to b 3 true to their principles. Mr. T. Simister having thanked the Lecturer and Chairman for their serviees, proposed that .the hearty thanks of the meeting be given them.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Possnett seconded the resolution with pleasure. He had listened very attentively to the lecture and though the utterances of Mr Stafford could not make him more nonconformist, still it would stir and stiffen him up a bit. (Hear, hear.) He was a Methodist of the 3rd generation and was now bringing up the 4th. (Laughter and hear, hear.) Mr Posnett also paid a Mgt, compliment to the Chairman. The Rev. C. W. L. Christien put the motion to the meeting and it was carried with acclamation. Both gentlemen having responded, the Chairman an- nounced that the collections during the anniversary had reached £65, the amount required by the fria nds,a state of things which was very gratifying'. The meeting concluded after the singing of several pieces by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>...._,,1ii._........ RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>...._,,1ii._........ RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>tBY A CORRESPONDENT.] During the week the Runcorn magistrates have been busy dispensing time, and five prisoners have been disposed of, four for periods of 3 months, and one for 6 weeks. The offenders committed were : three for larceny: and one each for wife desertion and begging. This shows a very high percentage of crime for the district ; but at the same time speaks volumes for the efficacy of the police supervision.</p>
        <p>I would. like to call the attention of the members of the Cemetery Committee to a small favour they might further confer upon their "customers." Yet, I speak not in the spirit of fault finding, for the way "Gods Acre "is kept and protected by them is highly creditable. My request is this time the mo- dest one that, say, two or more stead pipes might be erected at different points for the supply of water for the purpose of watering plants and shrubs on the several graves. They could be erected too with a view to convenience when the extension of the cemetery has to be brought about.</p>
        <p>Cycling is no doubt an exhilarating exercise, and a pleasant pastime, but when it is practised to the positive danger of pedestrians it is apt to become a nuisance. For some time past, strong com- plaints have been prevalent in Runcorn, in respect of those cyclists who have earned for themselves the unenviable notoriety of "scorchers." Norman road (perhaps one of the pleasantest promenades in Runcorn) is nightly the scene of furious cycle riding, which is calculated to endanger the lives of passers-by if not immedi- ately put a stop to. To ride a machine at a pace approaching that of an express is certainly a very serious cptravention of" our local bye-laws," and the polio e authorities of Runcorn, always on the alert to safe-guard the interest of the townpeople, should direct their attention to the escapades of these dangerous cyclists, and this before any serious accident calls for notice.</p>
        <p>lam given to understand that the committee having the Runcorn Heath and Common under con- sideration, met on Thursday morning, and decided in favour of a scheme for its conversion into a park and pleasure ground, submitted by Mr. James Wild- ing (surveyor). Roughly speaking the scheme is said to embrace a cricket and recreation ground of about eight acres, and a park planted with trees and shrubs containing a lake of two acres dimen- sions, constructed especially with a view to the winter pastime of skating, around which there will be a walk and seats and every facility afforded for the youngsters to sail their boats in summer time.</p>
        <p>Another very interesting piece of informatiqn that has come to my ears is the intimation that Sir John Brunner, Bart, M.P., has cemented to offer six scholarships to the children of Runcorn. The precise nature of the scholarships I have not yet ascertaineil, but believe they are highly valuable ones.</p>
        <p>SUMMER SALE.</p>
        <p>J. AND W DUTTON,</p>
        <p>16, 18, AND 20, SANKEY ST.,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MONDAY, JULY 17TH,</p>
        <p>1899.</p>
        <p>AND CONTINUING UNTIL END OF MONTH.</p>
        <p>REDUCrIONS</p>
        <p>IN ALL DEPARTMENTS.</p>
        <p>PEARL ASSURANCE COLLECTORS' PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The collectors of the Pearl Assarance Company,within the Runcorn and Widnes dietricts,on Wednesday partook in a delightful picnic to Freodsham, promoted by their superinteadent Mr. T. Gerrar 1 (Runcorn), and though the weather was not quite as desirable as could be wished the:party thoroughly enjoyed themselves. A capital knife and fork tea was provided at Mersey View. After the tables were cleared, an impromptu meetiag was held, Mr. Gerard being voted to the chair. There were also present amongst the company: Messrs T Evans (assis- tant superintendsnt), Frodsham ; J T Tracey (assistant superintendent), Widnes; W B Brimelew, M McGrath, J. Fitzsimmons, D B Dutton.—The Chairman said he was very pleased to meet them all again, but expressed sorrow at the inclement state of the weather, and likewise at the absence, unavoidealy, of several of their brother-collectors. Giving them a com- parison of the two years 1899 and 1893, he said within their ewn district for the March quarter 1898, £292 lie 50 was collected; an.l for the correspending quarter 1899, £371 is id. (Hear, hear). lii June quarter 1398 £337 7s 511 was collected ; whilst in the June quarter just ended £441 is 81 was the figure. Those figures spoke for themselves, and showed that they were making substantial increase. (Hear, hear.) According to promise made at their last annual gathering, that he would give a present to the one who made the greatest collection, he now had great pleasure in announc- ing his gratification at being able to hand over a bsautiful brief-bag to Mr. McGrath. (Hears hear.) He would say, however, that Mr. McGrath had been run rather closely by Mr. W. B. Brimelow. (Applause). In fact, Mr. Brimelow, had made the biggest collection, but his debit was greater than Mr. McGrath's.—Mr. McGrath, in accepting the present, said, as he had never spoken in public, they must accept his best thanks in the humble way he gave it them. He could only assure them that he and his fellow collectors would always d 3 their bast to forward the interests of the Pearl Assurance Co. (Hear, hear.) —Mr. Gerrard here, observed that the figures were M. McGrath's. £63 8s 9i; and Mr. W. Brim slow, £65 151 101 d. But Mr. Brimelaw'e debit being bigger than Mr. McGrath's, left the latter a little in front when the deductions were mule. Whilst he was on his feet, there was one thing he would ask as a favour, and that was te record 53 increase each month. It was not a big item, but it would require working for. He weuld also give a further present to the collector who did the most ordinary business in the next six months. (Applause). He took that opportunity also of extending to Mr. Tracey a hearty welcome to their district. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Tracey thanked them all for the reception accorded him, and though he was not in the position' to do as Mr. Gerrard had dole, he was sure that when they in Widnes reached anything like the fieure he hoped to reach, the presents would not be behindhand. (Hear. hear.) He hoped to see an increase in the Industrial Branch also. (Ipplause.)— Mr.T. Evans then stated that in accordance with his in- timation that he would present something to the second best he had great p (sumo in presenting Mr. W B Brimelow with a good all English made brass ink stand with cut glass bottles, and hoped that the recipient would live long to use it in the drawing up of policies. (Applause.)—Mr. W B Brimelow said he was much obliged fer the present. He had no intention of making any remarks but was much obliged to Mr. Evans.—The, company then adjourned and were photographed. Afterwards dancing and singing took place, the return journey being made about 9 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>HALTON LADIES CLUB.</head>
        <p>HALTON LADIES CLUB.</p>
        <p>82nd ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The members of that flourishing' and somewhat unique organisation, the Ladies Club Halton, celebrated their 82nd anniversary on Monday. There was a good at. tendance, and the usual routine business was transacted in the schoolroom. The members proceeded to the Parish Church, when the Rev. G. D. Wray, vicar, con. ducted a short and appropriate service, and the Rev. S. Wilkinson, viCar of Tim parley, preached an excellent sermon from St. Luke 13a. 21v. While the service was proceeding, rain descended in torrents, but the weather cleared up subsequently, and the usual parade of the village WAS made to the music of Runcorn Volunteer iland. In front of the band walked the Rev. S Wilkinson, Rev G D Wray, Dr Carruthere Medical Officer, Mnssrs H Ganion and T Wright, trustees, and Percy R Wright, secretary. Lady Brooke headed the ladies. A return was made to Halton Castle Green, where in a large marquee about 200 members sat down to an excellent repast, Lady Braoke presiding at one of the tables.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright gay., a hearty welcome to Lady Brooks once more to their midst and made apologies for the ab- sence of Mr. and Mrs. C. Reynolds who were absent in Scotland and Mrs. Wray who was unwell. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lady Brooke said it was now three years since she was present at their gala day and very glad she was to meet them again and to learn that the club continued to flourish as she hoped it might ever do. It was ten years since she came to Haiton—that was a good slice out of one's life— and while then she did not know anyone now she was amongst many friends. She was sure they would all bear her out when she said what an untold blessing the club had been to the village, and what a comfort it had been to be able to fall back upoa the sick allowance, without which many of them would have been hard put to it. She had sometimes heard a little grumbling as to the amount of the quarterly payments, but so far from being t)o high tho London authorities told them that were not the members unusually healthy and long lived, they would have to increase them. She trusted they might long keep up their superiority in this respect and that they would all enjoy this the 82nd anniversary of their friendly society and spend a happy afternoon and evening. (applause.) For several years now the club had had no high stewardess and she proposed that Mrs. Carruthers should fill the position if she would accept it. Mrs. Booth seconded the motion which was carried by acclamation.</p>
        <p>Mr. (+mon proposed a vote of thanks to the Rev. S. Wilkinson for his sermon and to Mr. Wray for &amp;lowing the use of the schoolroom and library and his kindness in every way.</p>
        <p>The vote ;vas duly accorded, am' brief addresse3 were delivered by bath the rev. gentlemen. At the call of Mr. Wright cheers were given for the honorary members, Lady Brunner having recently joined their ranks, and sent a cheque for£2. Mr Wright added that the annual payment ot members towards the management fund was 01 per year; this meant a total contribution of about £5 to pay secretary's salary, print- ing, sc., and was only equal to about one half of the expenses of the club. The other halt was made up by honorary subscribers. The Quinquennial examination of the clubs had just been made and the London gentleman who had had a hand in the valuations was not satisfied. He said the club paid out a great deal too much money for the amount of the contributions. For sickness the club paid out 6i for first thirteen weeks, and 33 per week for the second thirteen weeks and the valuer thought the last sum should be reduced to is 61 per week. Be did not think the management would submit to that. The club bad gone on improving year by year. In 1888 the funds of the club amounted re a little over £7OO, equal to £4 for each member. Now the, club's funds amounted to £l5OO, which meant about £7 per member. This was very gratifying. In another ten years there was every likelihood that the club fund would equal £lO per member. He thought the affairs of the club were very satisfactory. (Applause.) Mr. Gamon announced the death in America of Mrs. Hill, who bad been connected with the society for 58 years, and had only received one week's pay. She died on June 20.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Wray, a cordial vote of thankil was accorded to the trustees, Mears. Wright and Gamon, and both gentlemen re3ponded. Subsequently the members engaged in an old English dance, in which Lady Brooke todt part. At dusk the Castle grounds were illuminated. The catering, was ably carried out by Mr. and Mrs. Walker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD,</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the board was held at the Town Hall, on Thursday evening last week. Present : Messrs E Imison (in the chair), James Pritchard, F J Norman, W Handley. S Taylor, J Littler,J W Lightbura (clerk), And A Lightbura (assistant clerk.) Mr. Imison explained that Canon Woad was in Lon- don, and it therefore devolved upon him to take the chair.</p>
        <p>TRH LOAN FOR GRANVILLE SCROOLS..--THE INSTRUCTION _</p>
        <p>OF PUPIL TEA.CHERS. The School Management Committee recommended that the Schools of the Board be closed for the summer holidays from July 20th to August 21st. Tbe committee 'resolved that the Bethesda school be hired at 22a. 6d. per week, incluaing lighting and cleaning, during the alteration of Granville-street school ; also that applica- tion be made to the Public Works Loin Commissioners for a loan of £l,BlO, to be repaid in a term not exceed- ing 30 years, at 22 per cent., to meet the expense of the alteration of Granville-street school:</p>
        <p>Mr. Inaison moved the confirmation of the minutes. He said the Board as a body were exceedingly anxious to make provisisn for the instruction of pupil teachers at the Board Schools. They had reason to believe that some parents were unaware of the fact that this was the ordinary method by wh!ch children entered the teaching profession, which was an employment profitable, and undoubtedly highly honourable. The Basra would like to encourage, and make whatever arrangements they could, to make it easy for children to be introduced into the profession.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pritchard seconded, and tir motion was carried. THE WAKEFIELD tASE,</p>
        <p>The Clerk referred to the, statement made by a bey named Ernest Wakefield at an inquest held oa the 27th June, that he did not attend school, and that the school attendance officers had failed to look the case up. The Clerk now stated that the boy being over 13 years of age was outside their control.</p>
      </div>
