      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SMART CAPTURE OF A BICYCLE THIEF AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Monday morning, before the Mayor and other magistrates. Thomas Costello (15), living at Old Swan, Liverpool,was charged with stealing a lady's safety bicycle, of the value of £l2 10s., from the platform of the Bank Quay Railway Station. The Chief Conszable said the Railway Company wished for a remani in this case until Friday. The bicycle was stolen on Saturday night. It was in transit from Birkenhead to Lockerbie in Scotland, and should have been dispatched by the 9.55 p.m. train via Wigan. At 9.30 William Bennett, a ticket collector,caw the prisoner wheeling the bicycle through the ticket barrier when he asked him where he was taking it and be replied " To the luggage office for a porter," and was allowed to proceed. Later on P.C. Woods saw the prisoner in Liverpool-road trying to ride a bicycle, and as he did so in a very clumsy manner his suspicions were aroused and be went up to him and interrogated him. He was unable to give satisfactory replies, and the prisoner was locked up, inquiries showing that the machine had been stolen from the station. It appeared that one or two bicycles had mysteriously dirappeared from Bank Quay Station, and the officials wished for a remand in this case to enable them to make inquiries into the matter.—Asked whether he was willing to be tried here or at Liverpool the prisoner, with a smile on his face, replied " Here, sir."—The case was then re- manded until Friday. THEFT OF LEAD FROIII WHITECROSS WORKS. - . _</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Warrington Borough Police Court, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Henry Jones, a carter, of 35, Bing-street, was charged with stealing a quantity of lead, the property of the Whitecross Com- pany, Limited. He pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Con- stable informed the Bench that on Saturday P.C. Smith met the prisoner in Dallam-lane carrying a sack. He became suspicious, and asked him what he had in his bag, and he replied " nothing." Such an answer was absurd, and the Constable felt in the hag and found it to contain the lead produced. He questioned the prisoner se to how he came into possession of it, and as his answer was un&amp;atisfactory he was brought to the Police station. At first the prisoner gave the name of Smith, and afterwards that ef Jones. He subsequently said that a man named Linaker had given him the bag to carry it to the marine store dealer. It was afterwards found to have been stolen from a shed on the premises of the Whitecross Company. Limited. It had been bought by the com- pany of Mr. Thonlas Birchen, J.P. and he identified the lead as that which he had sold to the Company. The prisoner had told him (the Chief Constable) that he was going to plead guilty.— Mr. Thomas Birchall identified the lead as that sold by him to the company. It was part of an old cistern from his house.—Llewellyn Davies said that the value of the lead would be about 7s or 88.—Prisoner now stated that he was walking along the street when he met the man named Linaker, who asked him to carry the bag for him. He said be would do so for a short distance. When he turned to rest Linaker bad disappeared,and the Constable then came up.—The Chief Constable said the man Linaker had been seen by the police, but it was clear be had nothing to do with the robbery. He was nowhere near the prisoner when he was apprehended.— Mr Birchall said that the Whitecroes Company were being constantly robbed by cartes like the prisoner. The lead in question was from 15 to 20 yards away from the spot where the carter was at work.—The Bench decided to send him to prison for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>"A SCOURGE TO ALL."</p>
        <p>Sophia Eggingt—on-,-Wi-n-g at--gLikey Bridges, was brought up at Warrington Police Court on Saturday,be- fore Mr. Birchall and other magistrates, charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Samuel-street at 8.15 on the previous evening. She pleaded guilty to having had some drink, though she said she was not drunk.—P.C. Woods said he was asked to go to Samuel- street in consequence f the conduct of the defendant, who was drunk and making use of very filthy language. Her husband and her son-in-law endeavoured to per- suade her to go into her house, but without avail. Wit- ness also asked her to do so, when she used most abusive lacguage. She was a moat troublesome woman. —Mrs. Rigby, a neighbour, said that durisg the after- noon the defendant's daughter came to witnees's house. She was crying bitterly,and complained that her mother had throo n a bucket of water over her. She saw the defendant, who was in drink. Throughout the after- noon her conduct was beastly. She would not like to repeat the language she used.—Joseph Robinson, the de- fendant's son-in-law, gave similar evidence. He said he had to send for the police.—The Chief Constable : This woman is a scourge to all belonging to her; her husband is a foreman roller at Messrs. Monks Hall and Company's Works. He is a very respectable man, and he has done all he can to reform her. She was here on the 19th June last on a charge of having been drunk and disorderly, and fined 53 and costs. The magistrates told her that if she was brought before the Bench again she would be com- mitted to prison without the option of a fine. She took a solemn pledge to be a teetotaler for twelve months, and this is how she kept at.—The Chairman : I know abs is a terrible woman, and I wish the Inebriates Asylum were in existence, so that we could send her to sae ef them, where she could not get drink.—The Chief Constable said the woman had been three times before the Court that year. She was a terror to all belonging to her, and a constant source of misery to them.—D2- fendant : It's not right to give my daughter all the money.—The Chairman: I don't wonder he does not give it to you, because you are not trustworthy.—De- fendant said that if the Bench would liberate her on that occasion she would leave the town.—The Chief Con. stable : Her promises are very plentiful.—The Chair- man : Yes, as plentiful as blackberries.—She was sent to prisen for one month with hard labour. TERRIBLE BRUTALITY TO A WIFE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Poli-ce--CO-nri,--i;C:ri-iesday, before Messrs. W. Timmins and J. Ockleston, William Milling- ton, a waterman, was charged under a warrant with having on the 22nd April rim away from Runcorn, leav- ing his wife and two children chargeable to the common fund of the Runcorn Union. The prisoner said his wife left him—Thomas Cooke, relieving officer, said the prisoner had lived in Grice'a-square, wellington-Areet. On the 24'h April he was called to see prisoner's wife, and !oued Mae was near her confinement. The house was in. a destitute state. The window panes and even the window frtmes were smashed, and the furniture was thrown up and down the place. The woman told him that on the Siturday night previous the prisoner -while drunk had brutalY assauled her, and broken almost everything in the place. There was no food in the house, and he (witness) provided everything that</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
