      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>was necessary for herself and children. Next day the woman was confined. Dr. Robinson had been in attendance, hut on his advice Dr. McDougall was called in and the latter ordered the removal of the children to the Workhouse, which was done. The woman became deranged and also suff:rsd fro.n puerperal fever and nurses were en- gaged to attend her night and day. Uuder the care and skilful treatment of Dr McDougall she seemed to get batter and a month later she was removed to the Work- house Hospital. Since then she had beconse worse mentally and had been rsmovel to Chester Asylum and it was probable she would nev-sr be right again. Up to the present the Guardians had expended £lO. The ' prisoner had never mode the slightest inquiry with re- snect to his wife and he was found in Liverpool earning j6a ro.r day loading flats with timber.—Dr McDougall ! said be wa‘ medical officer of healch for the township of Runcorn under the Union. He found the prisoner's house in the condition des- cribed by Mr Cooke. The prisoner had deserted his wife !ravine her a legacy of a good many thumps. When he first saw the woman she was in an exhausted condition suffering from puerperal fever evidently the outcome of this ruffian's (prisoner's) misconduct and she was absolutely destitute. The neighbours were very kind. In reply to Mr. Johnson (magistrates clerk) Dr. McDougall said the prisoner never came to the house to maka an inquiry. He wished he had and h 9 would have landed him for assault. He had no doubt the woman's present condition was due to the prisoner's bad treat- ment. The woman told him that since her marriage the pegsoner had persistently abused and neglected her. j —Mr. Ockleston : She could not possibly have left him, jas he states. Dr. McDougall : Left him! she could not j crawl.—P.C. Fry deposed to apprehending the prisoner Jin Liverpool. He was going under the name of Mr. McCorrnack.—P.S. Brewster spoke to being, called to the prisoner's houss several times on the night of the 22ad April but each time the prisoner had mada his eicapet He had wrecked the house and beaten his wife.—The prisoner was sentenced to three months hard labour. Mr. Timmins expressei regret that the Bench could not make it more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MINOR CASES. '</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. '</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT,</p>
        <p>... FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Alderman Roberts Councillor Smethurst. Dr. Gornall, and Mr. James Hep herd. NEIGHBOURS' QUARREL.—Margaret Wells, 99, Winwick- rord, summoned Emma Hackett. 95, Winwick-road. for assault on the 27th Jule. There was a cross-snmmons for assault on the same date.—Mr. Browne appeared for th complainant and Mr. Forshaw represented the defendant.— Mr. Browne said Mrs Wells was the wife of Peter Wells who was employed at Messrs. Walker's Brewery and had lived at the same house) 19 years. :Defendant had been constantly annoying complainant and other neigneurs. On the day in question Mrs. H,.ckett was quarrelling with a Mrs. Leftwich, and such dreadful language was being used that Mrs. Wells went to bring away her little boy who was listening. Mrs. Hackett therefore turned upon her and seized her by the throat with her head against the wall.— Evidence was called in support.— Mr. Forahaw contended it was six of one and half a dozee of the other. Mrs. Weils interfered between Mrs. Hackett and Mrs. Leftwich and pushed the former who then pushed Mrs- Wells, The case had bean much exaggerated.— witnesses were called for this side of the case but the magistrates did not think It neeessary to hear all of them and dismissed the case, ordering each to pay their own costs. AFTeR ONE YEAR'S MARRIED LlFE.—A.lort Bent, of 46, Kerfoot-street. was summoned for assaulting his wife on the 17th June.—The complainant said the bot Per arose about money matters, and after some words between them the defendant thumped her. They ha I only been married a year and it had been going on all the time.—Defendant said he only pushed her off a chair.—The Chairman said they were very foolish young people quarrelling like that, but he hoped' defendant would treat his wife better. He was fined In the costs or 7 days. AN UNUSUAL APPLICATION.—The licensee of the Rose and Crown Inn, Bridze-street,"made application for an extension of time next Thursday.—Asked by the Bench what the occasion was, applicant said he did not kaow at present.— The Chairman told him he had better make up his mind before making application, as they coald not grant an extension without some specific reason.</p>
        <p>SITIMDAY.—Before Messrs. T. Birchall (in the chair,) R. T. Fairo'ough, and d. W. Hayward, A SATISFACTORY,REEITLT.—The case of Thomas Rutter v. Mary Alice Rutter, husband and wife, which had been ad- journed for a month, again came up for hearing. but the complainant was absent.—The husband. in answer to the Bench, said he had not asked his wife to keep away.—The Chairman. Did you sign the pledge ?_.Defendant: I did. —The Chief Cmstable : And have von kept it P—Defendant: Yes. sir.—The Chairman : And how have things vile on during the past mouth ?—Defendant • Quite differently. Of course there have been times when I have had to quiet her temper.—The Chairman : It is said that it requiries two to make a quarrel. Don't let her quarrel with you. Walk out of the way when she begins.—Defendant : I think that will be the best way.—The Chief Constable: Yes, walk out of the way when there is a storm brewing. (Laughter.)— The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before the Mayor (Alderman Wright). Alderman Blackhurst, Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst, and</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Birchall. THE EXTENSION OF TIME IT THE ROSE IND CROWN INN Mr Browne, solicitor, made an application, on behalf of the Warrington cabmen. He said an application for an extension of time (two hours) came before the magistrates a few days ago, but it was rather bungled The occasion was the annual meeting of the cabmen of Warrington, followed by a supper or dinner. The cabmen could not get to the Rose and Crown in Bridge-street, where the proceedings were to be held. until ten minutes to eleven. The business meeting would last three-quarters of an hour, and they wished the extension to be for two hours.—The magistrates granted until one o olock on Thursday. DRUNK IN CHlRGE.—Joseph Hampson, 8, King-street 'bus driver . was charged with having been drunk in charge of two harees and a 'bus in Buttermarket-street on Monday, the third inst.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Monks stated that at 10-30 p.m. on the 3rd inst. he saw a 'bus outside the King's Arms. There was no driver, and he waited a ehort time but no driver came. Mr. William Con- nor came down the street, and wanted to know where the defendant was. He wag at tench found in the King's Arms drunk. At ten o'clock he had both horses down, and one of them was not fit to work.—P.C. Leonard also gave evidence —Samuel. Hope,the condnctorlof the bus said lee fetched the defendant out of the King's Arms. He theught he Was capable of taking charge of the heraes.—Superintendent Monaghan said he saw the defendant drunk. dr. Connor, jun., told witness that the defendant had had the horses down.—The defendant said he was buying a cigar at the King's Arm'.—The Chief Constable: This is the first we have heard of the cigar. It took the defendant 20 minutes to select it. (Lauehter.)—Mrs. Plinston. liceneee of the King s Arms, was called. She said that the defendant had only one stout at her house. When he came in the last time it was for a cigar.—The Mayor said it was a very seri- ous matter to be drunk in charge of horses, and people in the 'bus. The least the Bench could do was to fine him 10s including costs. He had been cautioned before, and he hoped this would be the last time it would be necessary to do SO. PITCH AND Toss.—Joseph Hatton, 192, Liverpool-road.and William Tomkinson, 194, Liverpool-road, were charged with playing pitch and toss on Sunday. the 2nd inst., at the back of Liverpool read.—Defendants pleaded guilty.—P.O. Wood gave evidence as to seeing defendants playing at the game. One of the defendants said they were not tossing for money. They tossed a coin up to see which way they should go. be- cause one of them wanted to go one way, and the other another way.—The Chief Constable said that was only an ex- cuse. There had been another big batch of offenders, and the case would be heard next Monday.—Fined ss, including costs.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. Birchall (in.the chair), Councillor</p>
        <p>Shaw Green aud Mr. R. Garnett. "SELLING LACES."—Jarnes Gibson was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street. the previous night about 8 o'clock.—P.C. Carter said defendant had a few laces in his hand and when people refused to buy he used bad language. Witness had received several complaints in St. James'-street about the man's con.luct. Prisoner had no hawker's license —Committed for seven days in default of a fine of be and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before -Messrs. Charles Reynolds (presiding)</p>
        <p>and A. R. Norman..</p>
        <p>LICENSING.—Temp-o;1;37- authority was granted Martha Smitham, to sell at the Cornwall and Dev anshire Arms, lately occupied by Sarah Ann Urmston, and to Archibald Hainiag to sell at the Royal Oak, Weston, lately occupied by Geo,ge Walters. —The police raised no objection. RECOVERING MB OWN ORANGE.—ArnoId Unsworth. Sea- men's outfitter. Waterloo-road, Runcorn. made application for £1 6s 9d, the money being portion of £3 les ontained from him by fraud by a man named John Clarke. alias Rooney. who it will be remembered was sentenced to 6 months' imprisonment at the Cheshire Quarter Sessions Superintendent Pearson stated that the money was found on the man and also a gold watch. The watch was however his own and no application was made with regard to it. —The application was granted. FOOTBALL IN THE STREET ON SIINDA.Y.—William Fletcher, William Clarke, Job Nield, and Christopher Inman. youths above the age of 17 years, were summoned for playing football In Egerton-street on the 2nd of July, to the danger and annoyance of passengers.—The defendants all pleaded guilty.—P.C. Thomas S. Bowyer, said about 8.33 on the Sunday night in question he saw the four youths playing football with others. They were kicking the ball from Egerton-street into Brisco-street.—The Chairman observed that the practice was a great nuisance. Inasmuch as it was not the first case which had been before the Bench, they would be fined 4s 6d or 7 days. --The money was paid. "A PRovORED ASSAVIM."—Georo Cornelious Duckett of Regent-street, was summoned for assaulting Thomas Jones. in the employ o! George Duakett as a fish hawker on the 3rd Inst. —Defendant pleaded guilty.—Thomas Jones said the assaalt consisted of three blows be received from the defend- ant as he was going up Egerton-street.—Sarnuel Worrell, carter in the employ of the Gas Company, deposed to seeing the assault and hearing Duckett say to the complainant -If you don't come past this shop with civility I will foul your ear-hole"—Duckett stated that he did hit Jones, but he was aggravated to the assault by the insulting language he used to his miscue every time he passed the shop. He could pro- duce witnesses to prove that the young man Jones had challenged him to fight.—The Magistrates' Clerk observed that if the defendant was going to produce witnesses he should not have pleaded guilty.—The Chairman said Duckett had no possible right to take the law into his own hands, an i if he did he would certanly have to pay for it. Fined 5s and 13s 6d costs.—Duskett then asked what he was to do, to pro- tect himself.—The Clerk advised him to see the superintene ant of tbe DRUNK AND Dr9ORDERLT.—Arthur Rose, of Gilbert-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Mill-street on the Bth of July.—He-plea•ied -gtailty.—P.C. Wilson said about 7.30 hewas c tiled into Mill-street, and saw the defend- ant there with a crowd of people around. He was drunk and wanted to fight with a man named Collins. He subse- quently locked him up.—Defendant made a statement in which he alleged ibeing brought to Collins' house hy the daughters, whom ke t•eated, afterwards the son of Collins assaulted him, after which he struck Collins.—Fined 5s and 133 6d costs.</p>
      </div>
