      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE AT HILL CLIFFE.—OII Sunday afternoon there was a large congregation assembled in Hill Cliffe Chapel to take a part in the memorial service to the late Mrs. Catherine Baxter, of Southport, who died whilst visiting at her daughter's home (Mrs. Bennett) in Arpleyestreet, somewhat suddenly on Satur- day morning, July 2nd, and was buried at Hill Cliffe on the following Tuesday. The deceased lady was the relict of the late Mr. Thomas Baxter, Acton Grange, a former and highly respected trustee of Hill Cliffe. She was 75 years of age, and though of recent years she lived at Southport, she spent the greater part of her life in connection with Hill Cliffe, and was always present at the annual and other gatherings of a special:character—being present at the last anniversary services on June 10th. Mrs. Baxter was a most estim- able Christian lady beloved by all who knew her. The Rev. J. S. Hughes, pastor preached an appropriate and impregsive sermon from I Cor. xv. B. testifying to the loss which as -a church they felt they had sustained by the death of so devoted, so faithful, and so beautiful a supporter, Suitable hymns were sung. OPEN-AIR SERVICES IN THE GRAVEYARD.—The second service in the Hill Cliffe graveyard was held on Sunday night. The pastor, who has promoted these services,has not as yet had'an ideal evening for the purpose. The first occasion on which a service was held, the weather was doubtful and on the second the weather was So unfavourable as to make it impracticable and uhwise to go out, so the service was held in the chapel where a large congregation assembled. Last Sunday a storm was threatening all day and made an open-air service in the evening very uncertain, and this deubtless prevented many coming,but a goodly company gathered together, certainly more than double the usual congre- gation in the chapel, and a happy service was held, con- duetted by the pastor, Rev. J. S. Hughes. The service was limited to an hour and consistel of five hymns, two brief Scripture lessons,one of which,the first Psalm was recited, not read, two prayers and a short sermon suit- able and earnestly delivered on Mark i. 1. The singing is aided by a small American organ which is however not in good condition and another should be got and doubt- less will be before long. It would improve the singing if the congregation were not so scattered but mustered closer together. The volume of song was not so great as it ought to be from such an audience, but even as it was, the singing could be Ivard in Stockton Heath, and the voice of the preacher could be distinctly heard at a great distance. As a proof of the interest these ser- vices are awakening we may say that on Sunday even- ing, there were persons present from Widnes, Warring- ton, Appleton, Stretton, Hatton, Walton, &amp;c. Hymn papers were provided, and although no collection is taken, an opportunity is provided for all who wish to give an offering, so that the usual voluntary offerings in the chapel may not suffer in consequence of the change. The whole service was marked by heartiness, attention, and reverence.</p>
        <p>Ladies' Walking Shoes,Great Variety, SCALES AND SONS, E 3. Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Light Wines of Germany, so appreciated In the Summer Season, are in favour with the Medical Profession. " Zeller," from the Moselle district, is a wine light and palatable, and guaranteed absolutely pure. At the exceed- ingly low price of is a bottle, It is generally considered to be of wonit•rful value. It can be obtained of all W. &amp;A. GUSHY'S 3,000 Agents throughout the country.—Advt. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS Awn SHOES UNDER</p>
        <p>THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. -- -- POTTED TONGUE, PRESSED BEEF, POTTED HEAD, BOILED HAILS. all kinds of cooked meats. BAYLEY ISAlC.—Acirt. ASK or CUNNINGHAM HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt J. BAYLEY Isaac pays Special Care to the Quality and Cleanliness of Goods he makes.—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. Have you seen the NEW PORK SHOP that J. BAYLEY Is4.ac his opened in Horsemarket-street.—Advt.</p>
