      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NW BOOKS, MAGAZINES</head>
        <p>NW BOOKS, MAGAZINES</p>
        <p>ETC.</p>
        <p>MACMILLA.N AND CO.—Kipling is still the lion of I literature and enjoys a personal and professional ! popularity altogether unique. A few short months ago the world waited at his bedside, builetins frem the medical men attending the famous invalid were scanned in all the morning papers, and his ultimate recovery was, in England and America at any rate, looked upon as an event of national im- portance. People, and we know there are some, who were unacquainted with Kipling's writings, asked one another " who is this man ?" and being told they speedily atoned for the error in their literary education. Others had read Kipling but failed to appreciate him. The widespread sympathy he received in his illness was beneficial to them also; they were convinced against their own judgment—perhaps a hastily formed one—that Kipling had something in him. And being so con- vinced they dipped again into Kipling-lore and this time understood. To all these persons, and those too who have admired Kipliiag's stories since the first line flowed from his pen, the re-issue of his prose writings will be truly welcome. Messrs. Macmillan are placing on the market a Kipling prose library in ten volumes which is worthy of ' the highest encomiums. It is legibly printed on good paper, and bound in a style which will make it a handsome top shelf orna- ment. The ten volumes will be as follows : ' " Plain tales from the Hill:" "Life's Handicap," being stories of mine own people; " Many Inven- tions" ; " The Light that Failed" ; " Wee Willie Winkie," and other stories ; " Soldiers Three," and other stories ; " The Jungle Book," with illustra- tions; "The Second Jungle Book," with illustra- tions ; "Captains Courageous," with illustrations ; and" The Day's Work,' The first two volumes ars to hand and need no recommendation here. "Plain Tales from the Hills," and " Life's Handicap "have now a world-wide reputation and popularity. They are two of the most entertaining of the many books Kipling has written, and in the last named the reader first makes the acquaintance of those remark- able privates in Her Majesty's Indian Army. .Mulvaney, Learoyd, and Ortheris. We do not think tnere have been three such famous characters in fiction since Dumas created "The Three Musketeers," and both trios seem likely to win a well earned immortality. Volume 1 of the series contains, as frontispiece, an excellent pen and ink portrait of the author himself by W. Strang.—Volume 6 of Messrs. Macmillan's Eversley edition of " The Works of Shakespeare," is now out of the press. Its contents are "King John," "Richard the Second," and " Henry the Fourth," parts 1 and 2. Only four volumes now remain to complete the edition—a fact which should not be lost sight of by those who desire to purchase it when complete.— Students of history in general and admirers of Bis- marck in particular ought to be grateful to Dr. Moritz Basch for his valuable biography of the Iron Chancellor. Dr. Busch has had 25 years official and private intercourse with Bismarck, and is in con- sequence able to write authoritatively, and, indeed, disclose some secret pages of Bismarck's history. The work, which is published in Great Britain at 10s. 6d. is voluminous, and dates from 1870. It has always been expected that when Bismarck died there would be a new light thrown on events which occurred in the history of his country, but which, while he lived, were kept in the background. Dr. Busch confirms to some extent this impression, and his literary labours should go far towards explaining the atti- tude which the great Chancellor took in connection with state matters, and which was not very cleat at the time. We strongly advise the careful perusal of the work, which is enriched by beautifully tone, portraits of Bismarck and his chronieler. Dr. Busch. —The last to hand in Messrs. Macmillan's sixpenny series is Mr, F. Marion Crawford's story " A Cigar- ette Maker's Romance." G..NEWNES AND CO.—This month we would draw attention to "The Book of Cricket,' a new gallery of famous players, edited by Mr. C. B. Fry. ,It is published in sixpenny fortnightly parts, and contains superb reproductions from photographs of the leading cricketers of to-day. A feature of the pictures is that the subjects are not photographed in the studio but on the field of play,and each bats- man or bowler is seen in the act of demonstrating his skill. Ranjitsinhji and "Johny" Briggs lead the way in Part I, which contains ten full page por- traits. The reading matter by Mr. Fry is as interest- ingly and brightly written as possible and we are confident that "The Book of Cricket" will be im- mensely popular with all sorts and conditions of men." CASSELL AND CO.—The current number of "Little Folks " commences a new volume, and with it is given a beautiful painting book for the young peeple,centaining numerous pictures in colours and copies for painting. The announcements for the new volume are very voluminous and include a serial by Mrs. Molesworth entitled " The Three Witches," which. will please the girls immensely. There is another one for the boys, "River and Forest" by Mr. E. S. Ellis which should also prove a greate attraction. Then there are to be "Strang Traveller's Tales," " Stories of Ben the Sailorman " extraordinary competitions, puzzles' pictures, and all manner of literary and pictorial treats for tbe juveniles. Fathers and mothers, who have not already done so, should order "Little Folks" at once.—W. Clark Russell's" List, ye Landsmen"! is the last to band of Messrs. Cassell's 6d. series of popular novels. —"The Quiver" for July contains the opening chapters of a new serial story entitled "Love-light," by M. H. Cornwall Legb.—Tbe forthcoming number of " Chums " will contain a special interview with Mr. M. A. Noble, the famous Australian cricketer.—Messrs Cassell and Company will shortly publish a sixpenny addition of " The Wrecker," by R. L. Stevenson and Lloyd Osbourne."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WIDNES GAS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES GAS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER RECORD BROKEN.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of Widnes Council,held at Widnes Town Hall on Tuesday afternoon, the Mayer (Alderman J. W. Wareing), presiding, the medical offiner reported that the birth-rate in the borough for June was 318 per 1,000, and the death-rate 15•3 per I,ooo.—The Gas and Water Committee reported that for the past year there was a profit on the gas undertaking, after defraying the cost of the public lighting (£1,579 4s 10d), of £3,499 5s 8d ; and on the water undertaking of £1,295 12s 31, and they recommended that from October Ist next, the price of gas be reduced to ordin- ary consumers by ld per 1,000 cubic feet, and that the quantity of gas suppliad per penny by prepayment meters be increased from 36 to 38 cubic feet.—Alderman Timmis proposed the adoption of the recommendation. —Councillor Davies seconded the motion, which was supported by Alderman Williamson., was carried unanimously. This reduction will bring the price of gas in Widnes down to le 3d per 1,000 cubic feet to consumers of over 3,000,000 cubic feet per annum, and to ls 51 per 1,000 cubic feet to other consumers, figures which have never been touched by any other public authority or gas company in the world.</p>
      </div>
