      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>WAR RINGTO N ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>IA meeting of th;s committee was hem in the Bryird R( oni at the Workhouse on Fillay morninlz. Mr. G. Pillmg pins d,d, and there were preseut :—Messrt-. William Hickman, A .1 Jolley. A Jolley. Willi,,ni Houghton, J Clarke. T Halton, with J C Sutton (clerk), S St)tton (assistant clerk), William OS/MI (valuer), ani J L Tunstall (assistant oversecr.)—Mr. 0 wen reporte..l that since the last meeting he had made the following valuations :—ln the Tewnship of Warrington. The properties of the Cheshire Lines Committee,Warrington. These present asseisments will require to be struck out : Sables and yard near Lithgoes-lane, £6O gross ; £5l rateable; Central S*.atioa and Goods yard near Winwick- street, £1206 103 gross ; £lOB2 10s rateable. These present assessments will remaha unaltered : Tower and water tank near Winwick-street, £4O gross, £34 rate- able; goods offices, near Winwick-street, £6O gross, £5l rateable; office and weighing machine near Winwick-st., £lO gross,£B 103 rateable. New assessments on valua- tion made June, 1899 :—Yard and stables Lythgoes- lane, £6B gross, £6O rateable; offices occupied by Great Northern Company near Winwick-st. and near weighing machine £5 15s gross, £4 103 rateable ; Central Station, Winwick st., £440 103 gross, £399 rateable ; goods yard warehouse hydraulic power, buildings, fixed plant near Winwick-street.Lythgoes-lane,and Battersby-lane, £2256 gross,£l994 rateable. The Great Central Hotel,Winwick- street, Wairington,£loo gross,£Bs rateable. This hotel was occupied last week. The stables have not vet been erected. Messrs. the Warrington Brick and .Tile Works, Latchford, gross £196, rateable £157. Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, Bank Quay, additions and wharf, gross, £942; rateable, £683 ; pre- vious assessments, gross, £4,046 ; rateable, £3,334 ; total, gross £4,988 ; rateable, £4,017. In the township of Great Sankey Mr W D Houghton, Sankey Wire Mills. The present assessment of gross, £791 8s ; rateable, £614 15s should be struck out and a new assessment entered as gross, £853 183 ; rateable, £6.6 103. The increase in this assessment does not show the additions which have teen made to the work inasmuch as one process of manufacture having been relinquished ; credit had to be given for buildings, not now occupied and for others taken down. The Mersey White Lead Works, Saukey Bridges : the alterations here are still in progress and the valuation for assessment has not been made. In the township of Poulton-with-Fearnhead Mr Price appealed against this assessment of his new house : Gross, £45 rateable, £3B. He (Mr. Owen) was of opinion this assess- ment should be reduced to gross, £37 103; rateable. £32. The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>DISiRESSING SUICIDE OF A WARRINGTON MILKDEALER. Early on Friday moraing,Parah Whittaker, a widow, carrying on business as a milk-seller at 20,FenneI-3treet, Warrington,committed suicide under distressing circum- stances. It appears that she retired to rest at 10.25 on Thursday evening with her daughter 20 years of age. About 5.50 a.m. next morning William Cook, who was in the employ of the deceased and lives on the premises got up and on going into the back-yard saw the deceased woman crouching down in the raintub with her head partly out of the water. He gave an alarm and Sergeant Hollingworth and P.C. Corbett were quickly on the scene but life was found to be extinct. The deceased had been in a distressed state of mind for the last two years since the death of her husband. Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy ceroner, held an inquiry res- pecting the death of the deceased at the Parade Room - off the Police Station on Friday afternoon.—The first witness called was Sarah Whittaker, who said the deceased was her mother, and was the widow of Peter Whittaker, a milk seller. She bad been very low spirited, but witness had never beard her say she would take away her life. She retired to rest at 10.30, and when witness awoke she found her mother was absent. She afterwards saw her in the rain tub, but was not present when she was pulled out.— William Cook said he lived at the deceased's house, and on going into the backyard about six o'clock he saw . the deceased inside the rain tub. She was in a standing position inside the tub, with her head bent forward. The kitchen stool had been used by the deceased to get into the tub. She was in her nightdress at the time. The jury after a shert consultation, returned a verdict that deceased committed suicide whilst of unsound mind, and expressed their sympathy with the relatives . of the deceased in their bereavement. A PADDINGTON Fat 31.'S CLAIM FOR BREACH OF CONTRACT. At the Warrington County Court last Thursday, before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, Messrs, W Royston and Co., of the Paddington Chemical Works, sued John Caldwell, Orford Green Farm, Orford, for damages for breach of contract in the sale and delivery of 33 tons 6cavt of straw at 22s per ton. It was contended that The straw delivered was inferior in quality to that contracted to be sold, and that the plaintiffs had suffered a loss on the re- sale of £25 us 2d,the claim with the costs amount- ing altogether to £2B is. There was a counter claim for Xll lOs against the plaintiffs for the price of two tons of straw sold by the defendant to the plain- tiffs.—Mr. Lorraine, barrister,(instructed by Messrs. Sandys and Williams,of Liverpoel),appeared for the plaintiffe,andMr.Browne for the defendant Caldwell. Mr. Maurice Hitchin Royston was the first witness. called. He stated that he was the representative of the plaintiff's firm.—in answer to Mr. Browne, he said that he swore before the Revising Barrister, at Padgate, that he was one of the proprietors of the firm. There was an arrangement to be made for him to become one of the proprietors, but it did not come off, but that was not a matter that affected the present case. What he said was not a matter of moment.—His Honour: It is a scandal to make such a barefaced statement in court.—Witness was cross-examined at considerable length by Mr. Browne as to where the purchase was made. At first he was understood to say that it was made at the farm, but being pressed, said it was made between the Griffin Hotel and the White Bull in Horsemerket-street, Warrington.—His Honour to witness: Presently you will show yourself utterly unworthy of credit.—Further questioned, witness said that Mr. Caldwell was a man of undoubted respectability, and that he was held in high repute amongst farmers generally.—A couple of men from ' the railway gave evidence as to the condition of the bales when they reacted the station. They both stated the bales were wet, and one man des- cribed them as being in some parts rotten.—Mr. Browne submitted he had no case to answer, for assuming there was a warranty, he conten- ded there bad been no proof of a breach of it. Evidence should have.been brought from Aberdeen to prove that the straw was bad there. There was no such evidence whatever. The defendant had re- fused to sell the straw time after time to Royston, and eventually meeting him in the Market Place agreed to sell it at 23s per ton. Against what he would say, they had the evidence of a man who had wilfully committed perjury before the revising bar- rister. He had no doubt His Honour would also take into consideration the way in which he had conducted himself in the box, in not answering questions. If he would tell a lie at one time, he would tell a lie at another. This was a trumped up tale and the case should never have been brought into court—Evidence was given by the defendant to the effect that he agreed to sell the straw at 235. per ton as it iftood, and he ab- solutely denied that he gave any warranty as to it being prime, bright oat straw. He personally saw the bales loaded and each time when it was raining the load was sheeted.—Evidence in support was given by a man employed on the farm of de- fendant.—His Honour in summing up, said he had decided between the evidence of Royston and the evidence of defendant. He could not rely upon the evidence of . Royston who seemed to be perfectly indifferent to the obligations of an oath. His manner in answering qestions entirely led him to reject his evidence and and accept the evidence of defendant who said there was no warranty because considering the payment by plaintiffs to defendant, it was not likely a man wouldi pay for an article without being satisfied with t. The correspondence seemed to bear out what defendant said namely, there was no warranty. In no single letter was he charged with not having given a warranty. He must therefore give credence to defendant's evidence and award mhi judgement non that point. They had no evidence as to how the straw was exposed on its voyage to Aberdeen the boat having to go round the North ot Scotland ; nor did they know how many jays it was at sea, how it, was treated, or what hap- pened to it. Why could not this damage have happened on the journey ? There was no evidence to satisfy him there had been a breach of the war- ranty as set up by plaintiffs. The time for the ex- amination of the straw should have been at War- rington as it was to be delivered there, and on that pint he gave judgment for defendant. With re- gard to the counter claim which was tor goods sold he would give judgment for the defendant.—Dur- ing the hearing of the case the counter claim was altered to £l3 163 Od.</p>
      </div>
