      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>11, SPLENDID</head>
        <p>11, SPLENDID</p>
        <p>NEW SERIAL STORY,</p>
        <p>ENTITLED !-</p>
        <p>" TUE REVENGE OF VALERIE,"</p>
        <p>BY HUAIB NISBET,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>AUTHOR OF</head>
        <p>AUTHOR OF</p>
        <p>"THE SAVAGE QUEEN," "THE DIVERS," " BAIL UP," "HUNTING FOR GOLD," "LIFE AND NATURE STUDIES," "EIGHT BELLS." " DR. BERNARD ST. VINCENT." ETC., ETC.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>SOMETHING ABOUT</head>
        <p>SOMETHING ABOUT</p>
        <p>"THE REVENGE OF VALERIE.;!</p>
        <p>"TIIE REVENGE OF VALERIE " is an exciting novel about Klondyke which has solid foundations of fact. Among MR. NISBET'S intimate friends is an expert gold digger from Australia who was one of the pioneers of gold-mining in British Columbia. and has kept in touch with the most stirring events and even the smaller details of existence out there, which both of them from their earlier Austral experi- ences are able to appreciate as no ordinary observer could. The love theme of the story is strong and the characters are drawn with skill and strength,induced by a study of human nature in many lands. Sir Lewis Eastlake is introduced to the reader, when, at twenty-four years of age, he is going the pace in a London Gambling Club. An altercation there causes a duel at Cherbourg in which his opponent is killed. Sir Lewis has been accompanied by his Club com- panion, Captain Eric St. Arnoul,who has him nursed at the house of a Cherbourg young doctor,an acquaint- ance of some years, with a pretty sister who does the -nursing. The story, however, centres on the passion of a woman for a man rather than on that of a man for a woman. The beautiful and wealthy Duchess Valerie has from motives of affection been finding Sir</p>
        <p>Lewis Eastlake with money through a financier, and when she learns of his illness, she arranges with her cousin, Captain St. Arnoul, for the invalid's removal to her seat at Calais,the Captain to be his escort and companion. She thinks that ncw it will all enil well, little imagining that Sir Lewis has before the duel been privately married to the doctor's sister, his engagement to her having been of some standing. Sir Lewis on his recovery goes alone to seek a fortune for himself and Wife at Klonclyke. and while he is encountering advaniures there, the Duchess, on a reaction of sentiment for him, cruelly plots to ruin the character of his wife, and so bring about a divorce to be followed by her own marrisge to hin. The Duchess attains her triumph, but the girl has a counter revenge which makes a striking climax.</p>
        <p>NOTE THE FACT! that this absorbingly interesting tale by a popular author, commences</p>
        <p>TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>?Letters to the Oitor.</head>
        <p>?Letters to the Oitor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in al cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. Matters intended for publication must b 3 written on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>OUR BATHS.</head>
        <p>OUR BATHS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Can you spare space for a word or two in sup- port of S.D's. letter re "Our Baths" in last weeks' " Ex- aminer." Sxpence is too much for most working men to pay for a wash bath, but when we on this side of the river have to add to that sum threepence for a return ticket across the bridge then our weekly bath cost,* us ninepence a sum that most working men cannot afford to pay for a wash. Now sir, I think it is a disgrace to a town with the population that Runcorn has, that there are no public swimming and slipper baths in the place. I believe there are about 4,000 deaths annually in the kingdom from drowning, and lam sure Runcorn has contributed its quota to that number. Surely it is not toe much to assume that the greater number of those deaths would not have taken place if a knowledge of swimming was more general among all classes of people. I contend that if our local rulers do not inqist on a bath being provided in every house (either cottage or man- sion) that is built within their district, then it is their duty to aee that a public place is provided where one oan have a wash or swim, and one's boys and girls can learn to swim at a reasonable price, and with compara- tive safety. I could say much more in support of " baths," but lam afraid to trespass ol your space. wellington-street, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>J. H.</p>
        <p>SUNDkY CDNCERTS IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you be so good as to insert the following letter in your issue of Saturday next? The sunject with which it deals is to my personal knowledge exciting considerable interest in religious circles in the town and there is a strong and growing public sentiment against the lotting of the Parr Hall for commercial or money making purposes on the Lord's Day.</p>
        <p>Ist July 1899.</p>
        <p>Dear sir, Re ILLEGAL SUNDAY CONCERTS, PARR HALL. I am in receipt af your letter of yesterday's date in which you state that the subject is under the control of the Parr Hall Committee and further that " if the con- certs are illegal the Mayor thiNks that your complaint should be addressed to the persons who propose to hold the concerts."</p>
        <p>With respect to the last point I may say that by the same post which conveyed my respectful protest to his Worship the Mayor, a letter was sent to the gentlemen who have announced these concerts pointing out their illegality and asking them to refrain from giving them. With respect to the subject being under the control of the Parr Hall Committee may I respectfully and courte- °ugly ask the following questions? 1. Is not the Parr Hall Committee a subcommittee of the Town Council which latter body is under the presidency of his Worship the Mayor ? A letter addressed to the Mayor on any subject affect- ing the government of the town should,. I apprehend, be sent by him, or by his legal adviser, to the proper quarter, and not referred back to the writer for direct communication with a sub-committee. 2. Is not the letting of the Parr Hall in the hands of the Town Clt-rk ? 3. Is not the Town 'Clerk the legal adviser of the Town Council, and of all the sub-Committees of the Town Council? Your letter implies that there is a doubt as to the ille- gality of the concert in question. 5. In view of the decision in the Court of Queen's Bench in 1392, and the subsequent proceedings in ?on- nection with the Home Office (of which legal proceedings sent a report to the Mayor), is it possible that you can doubt the illegality of concerts given on the Lord's Day for money payment? In writing to the Mayer, with respect to these illegal Sunday concerts, it was not, originally my intention to make the matter public, but in view of the evident re- luctance of the authorities to act according to the law, I reserve to myself the right of sending this letter to the press, and I shall endeavour by other means to do all in ray power to safeguard and preserve that great gift of I God to man—the Day of Rest.—l am, dear sir, faith- fully yours,</p>
        <p>EDWARD T. REED. J. Lyon Whittle, Esq., Town Clerk, Warrington. It may be further stated for the information of the general" public "that in the case of any entertainment given on Sundays for money payment, the keeper of the hall, the manager of the entertainment, the chair- man or president, the persons who collect or receive money or tickets, the persons advertising the entertain- ment. the printers or publishers of such advertisements render themselves liable to the heavy penalties imposed by the Act of Parliament. This mltter will not be allowed to drop for there are many who feel strongly that a pubrc body such as the Tewn Council, which is responsible for carrying out the law, should not themselves set the pernicious example of openly breaking it.—l am, dear sir, faithfully rours EDWARD T REED Emmanuel Church, Warrington, July 6th, 1899.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESVIWN TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>The following examination results have come to hand:—Steam : Walter W Thompson. 2nd A. Ap- plied Mechanics : Jaraes A Bullock, 2ad A. John P Ecicersley, Horace Williamson, 2au E. SALVATION ARMY, EARLESTOWN CORPS.</p>
        <p>On Friday and Saturday last, a sale of work was held in the Salvation Army barracks for the purpose of raising funds, to clear off some small liabilities, amounting to about £lO. There was a good stock of useful and saleable articles, and there was a very fair attendance of members and friends of the Army. The sale was opened on Friday by the Rev. P. R. Passmore, and on 'Saturday by the Rev. T. R. Matthews. There were special services conducted by Major and Mrs. Greenwood, of Liverpool. About £6 was realised by the sale, L which will be continued on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EARLESTOWN MISSION.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN MISSION.</p>
        <p>In connection with this Mission and on behalf of the choir picnic fund, a service of sacred song en- titled "In His Steps, or" What would Jesus do ?" was rendered in the afternoon and evening on Sun- day by an augmented choir and band conducted by Mr. John Potter. On both occasions the hall was well filled, and the service was exceptionally well given. The connective readings were clearly and impressively read in the afternoon by Miss Prescot and in the evening by Mr. J. Webster. The principal vocalists were Mrs Morris, Miss Scott,Miss Leyland, Miss Dickens, and Miss Lewis, Masters Macklin and Dring, Messrs W Brown, J J Leatherbarrow, J Ley- land, Ainsworth and Marsh. The instrumentalists were, organ, Mr E Kenyon; piano, Master E Smith; violins, Messrs Edmonds, Crompton, Bullock, J Hobbs, J Pilling and J Kenyon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>VIADUCT WORKS PAINT SHOP PICNIC.</head>
        <p>VIADUCT WORKS PAINT SHOP PICNIC.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the workmen employed in the paint shop at the Viaduct Works, to the number of about 70, had a picnic to Billinge. Leaving Earles- town in three wagonettes, they enjoyed the drive through the country and reached Bihinge in good time. The" Foot of the Causeway "inn was made the rendezvous, and on the green attached to the house games of bowls were indulged in, and a handicap was arranged, in which Mr.. Wyatt took first prize and Mr.P. Bowden, second prize. There was also a foot race organised,G. Bunting coming in first J. Anderton second, and H. Thomas third. An ex- cellent knife and folk tea was partaken of, after, which the members of the party passed the re- mainder of the evening in an enjoyable manner. The return journey was commenced about 10.30, and Earlestown was reached about midnight,the outing being much enjoyed.</p>
        <p>POISONING INCIDENT AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, Mr. W. Longton, a farmer at New- ton, and his wife,had a very unpleasant experience. They bad picked what they thought were very fine mushrooms in one of the fields, and were enjoying them at breskfast. A short time afterwards both were seized with a numbness about the legs and feet, which they attributed to the heat, but as the sensations increased and became painful, they became alarmed and sent for Dr. Watkins. It was then found that they were suffering from poiwning and immediate steps were taken by Dr. Watkins, Dr. Harold Watkins and Dr. Patterson to remove the poison. Mr. Longton was soon restored, but his wife was under the influence of poison for some time longer, although both are now thoroughly recovered. It is supposed what they thought were mushrooms, were really poisonous fungi.</p>
        <p>IMPUDENT ROBBERY FROM A TILL AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Arthur West, a bicycle fitter, and Arthur Mason, a labourer, were charged with stealing lie 04,1 in money from the till of a cash drawer in the shop 13, llaydock-et., Earlestown, at noon on Saturday, the property of Alice James, milliner. &amp;c. About 9.30 a.m. prisoner IMason went into the shop begging, and at 10 a.m. Ithe prisoner West also went into the shop to beg, and going outside joined Mason. They both stood at the window a short time,and were seen pointing in the direction of the till in the shop. Nothing more was seen of them, and at 11.15 a.m. Miss Statham, shop assistant, went into the shop and saw Mason leaning over the counter with his hand in the till, and on seeing her he ran out of the shop and jiining the prisoner West, they ran down back Bridge-street. Miss Statham followed, and called to some men to stop them. Mason was caught in Suez-strbec, and taken to the Police Station by Mr. T. Martindale,School Attendance Officer,and handed over to the police. Martindale then went out, and soon afterwards brought in the prisoner West and gave him into custody. Both men were charged with stealing from the till in the shop, but they made no reply. They were remanded until Monday next,</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LOD4E, No. 1644. A meeting of the above was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Wargrave-road, the programme being "Sisters Surprise." They did it effectually Sic Miss Warburton taking the chair at 8 o'clock and turning the brothers out of office appointed the following sisters: V.T. Sister Mrs Woods, P.C.T. Sis Mrs Williams, Chap Sis Miss Ord, Mar Sis Mrs Smith DM Sister Mrs Grant, sec. Sis Mrs Francis, F. see,. Sis Miss Hill, treasurer Sis Mrs Hall, guard Sis Mrs Lord. After the opening services a member was restored. Secretary read post card from Hope of Sutton Lodge who are having a picnic to Eastham. Arrangements were then made after a short discus- sion (in which Brothers Clarke, Francis, Woods,and Williams took part) to hold an open air meeting on July 24th at the corner of Brook- field-street. Brother Jones was granted a clearance card be removing to Ashton. The following items were rendered :—Pianoforte solo, Bro Willis; song, "Farmer's Boy," Bro Singleton; recitation, "The Owd Bellman," Bro Warburton; song, " Work," Bro Woods; short address, Bro Jones: song," Men of Britain," Bro Jones; reading, "A Fishing Yarn." Bro Newman; song, "Have you seen her," Bro Phillips; recitation, "1 had a dream," Bro H Hall ; short address, Bro Malley, of Progressive Mission Lodge,Liverpool ; mouth organ solo,Bro W Leather- barrow; sang, "Temperance Bells," Bro Clarke; short address, Bro Francis; address, Bro Lord ; song, Bro Williams. On the motion of Bro Clarke thanks were aocorded to the sisters for their surprise, prizes be- ing then awarded by Sister Warburton to those who had taken part, and the meeting closed at 9.50.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF ALDERMAN HARRISON.</p>
        <p>The death of Alderman James Harrison,which occurred on Tuesday, at Seuthport, removes one of the older generation of towns-people who had been associated with the government of St. Helens and public affairs for la great number of years. The deceased alderman was about eighty-seven years of age,but notwithetanding the decay of his physical powers he attended the meetings of the council regularly up to four months ago, and took a lively interest in the proceedings. At that time he went for a short visit to Soutbpert, where, shortly after his arrival, he was taken ill,and for the past two months had been confined to bed, under the care of Dr Thornton together with his can, Dr Harrison, of Stockport, and Dr H Knowles, of S;. Helene. Mr Harrison commenced his career at Lancaster, and then came to St. Helens as assistant to the late Dr Garton, and eixty years ago set up a chemist's' establishment in :et. Helens, occupying premises in Church-street and the neighbourhood of the Old Market Place up to a few years ago. He married Miss Fairclough, of Billinge, and had three children—viz., Miss Harrison, who resided with him, Dr Harrison, and Mrs Baggy, of Claughton. During the past four months he had resided at Burlington House, Portland-street, Southport, where death took place. Alderman Harrison was for some years a member of the Board of Commissioners. He was elected a councillor with Sir David (then Colonel) Gamble for Wind'e Ward. Sir David was elected the first Mayor of the Borough, sal Mr. Nebster an Aldermen, Mr. Harrison being a few days later elected councillor in succeasien to Mr. wet ster, and afterwards alderman Pe 1874. The de- ceased alderman occupied the Mayoral chair in the year Thee. Ho wes alderman for South Windle Ward. and was re-elected last Novemher fer a further term of five years. He was a member nf the boroueh bench, and for several years attencled so regularly every day to the duties ef the position that he became known to the court officials as" the stipendiary." The news was first e 7 Mr.J. Cross Eccles, rc solicitor,eived in received dtwHaselens on Tuesday T uewitahy by regret. The flag m at the Town Hall was placed half mast. Mr. Harrison wee a Churchman and a Conservative:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>GOSPEL HALL, FORSTER STRTET. On Sunday night Mr. J. Taylor gave an address on the Spititual Birthday, from the text, " How old art " Gen. 47-8. He could well remember the day 46 years ago, when he had a second birth. By nature, man is born in sin and a child of wrath. Unless a man is born again he can neither see ger enter into the Kingdom of Heaven. This statement was made by Christ, not to a deep dyed sinner, but to a most respectable man, a reli- gious synagogue ruler of the Jews, to prove that man naturally is unfit for Goi's presence, and he needs a new nature from God. This new nature is formed when a man accepts Christ as his Saviour. He is born again' into the fatally of God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CLUB FIXTURES.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CLUB FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>At the usual weekly meeting of the Committee, the fixtures of the club for next season were submitted. On the whole a fair list has been ccmpiled. From the table shown below it will be seen that there are a few open dates, particularly Good Friday. flaydook have got the first match at Haydock on Christmas Day, and visit Earlestown on New Year's Day. The signing on of players is still going on, an it is expected the club will be able to place a competent team in the field at the commencement of the season. LANCASHIRE LEAGUE FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>September.-2, Darwen, home; 9, Liverpool White Star, away ; 16, Southport, away; 23, Blackpool, home. October.-7. Darwen away ; 14, Blackpool, away ; 21, Horwich, honac ; 28, Liven:re! White Star, home. November.-18, Rochdale, away; 25, Wigan County, home.</p>
        <p>December.-2, Chorley, home; 9, Middleton, home; 23, Stockport, home ; 25 (Christmas Day), Haydock, away ; 26 (Boxing Day), South Liverpool, away. January.-1, Haydock, home; 6 South Liverpool, home ; 13, Horwich, away ; 20, Southport. home ; 27, Chorley, away. February.-3, Middleton, away; 10, Stalybrldge, home ; 27 (Shrove Tuesday), South Shore, home. March.-3, Crewe Alexandra, away; 10, Wigan County, home; 17, Stalybridge, away ; 24, Crewe Alexandre, home. _</p>
        <p>April.-14, Southport, away ; 1S (Easter Monday), South Shore, away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ANOTHER SCHOOLBOY DROWNED AT EAR LES rowN.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER SCHOOLBOY DROWNED AT EAR LES rowN.</p>
        <p>STRANGE CONDUCT OF BOYS.</p>
        <p>Another case of drowning took place at Earlestown on Saturday, making the second within a week. About 11 o'clock on Saturday morning, Amos Pybrous, 8 years of age, a scholar at Wargrave school, and son of George Pybrous, a machine man, of Manchester-road, Vulcan, was playing near his home, and half an hour later his father wanted him for something or other, but was unable to find him. Nothing much was thought of his absence just then, but as he did not turn up during the afternoon nor evening his parents became anxious. The boys with whom ha had bean playing in the morning were questioned, but thy denied any knowledge of his whereabouts. A search was then instituted, and the district round was scoured, but without avail, and noth- ing could be learned of the missing lad on Sunday, his playfellows stoutly declaring that they knew nothing of him. Henry Phytbian. one of the boys, 11 years of age was repeatedly que,tioned but he as often declared he did not know. About a quarter to six on Sunday even- ing a Mrs. Pritcllard was talking to Phythian about Pybr-ius and asked him where the latter V 7.49, and he then said he was in the canal, and added that they were playing on the swing bridge over the canal at Alder Root-lane and the bridge being a little open the deceased jumped and fell into the water. Mrs. Pritchard at once gave informa- tion and Thomas Screech Ark keeper,immediately went Ito the epot,and commenced grappling, and at 8.40 p.m. he recovered the body of the unfortunate boy. The back and legs were bruised, but this may have been I caused by a passing barge. An inquest was held at the Wargrave Inn, on Monday levening, by Mr. F. A. Jenea, deputy coroner. Evidence 'bearing out the above facts was given, and a verdict of " Accidentally drowned" was returned. The coroner took young Phythian severely to task, and pointed out to him what a shocking thing it was to know that the poor boy was lying in the canal, and yet not to tell his parents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>FATAL RAILWAY ACCIDENT AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>FATAL RAILWAY ACCIDENT AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>WARNING TO RAILWAY TRESPASSERS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night, Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, held an inquest at St. Helens Town Hall on the body of Walter Houghton. 18 years of age, of 230. Newtoa-road, Parr, who was killed by an express passenger train on the London and North-Western Railway near to the Bold Collieries, and St. Helens Junction, on Twsday afternoon.—Robert Douglas, a collier, said that he worked at the Bold Colliery and the youth Houghton also worked there. A • footbridge was provided for the men to cross the line by, and most of the colliers used the bridge, but a few of them did not. They were in the habit of crossing the metals. Witness crossed the bridge as a couple of trains were passing in opposite directions, and when the trains had gone he saw the youth Houghton lying at the side of the rails. He picked him up and the railway officials were informed.—James Lucas, station master, said that the body was removed on a bank engine to St. Helens Junc- tion station and was afterwards sent by passenger train to St. Helens. The youth was unconscious. At Sutton Oak station Dr. Bates, who had been telephoned for, joined the train,but the young man died before the train reached St. Helens. A footbridge was provided over the line near where the accident happened, but some of the colliers persisted in crossing the metals, although they saved no time by doing so. Detectives had been set to watch the place and there had been convictions against the colliers for trespassing on the line instead of using the bridge.—The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death," and the deputy coroner expressed a hope that the oc3urrence would be a serious warning to others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON' LIBERALS AND THE CLERICAL TITHES BILL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON' LIBERALS AND THE CLERICAL TITHES BILL</p>
        <p>There was an encouraging attendance at a special meeting of the General Council of the Warrington Liberal Association, held on Tuesday evening in the Committee Room of the Liberal Club. The meeting had been called at short notice, to consider the Clerical Tithes Bill, and a resolution was submitted condemning the action of the Government in this, their latest proposal, to legis- late for a privileged class at the expense of the general body of the taxpayers of the country. The meeting was held in camera but we understand that the chair was taken by Mr. Arthur Crosfield (in the absence of the president of the Association, Councillor John Crosfield). The first business was the election of officers. Mr. Arthur Crosfield was re-elected Chairman of the Executive Committee, and Alderman Henry R3berts, deputy Chairman. The Filmes Committee and Registration Committee were also appoiated. Apologies for non-ittendance were received from the Rev. W. H. Drummond, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, and Councillor Shaw Green.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews ?wrote:-9, Wash-la.ne,Latch- ford. Warrington. Dear sir,—My absence from home until a late hour to-morrow will prevent my being pre- sent at the Liberal meeting. My great regret is that the Tories are doing their utmost to facilitate the dis- establishment and disendowrnent of the Anglican Church. I say regret because I fear that august body is entertaining high hopes of holding on to the nation's property—hopes, I need hardly say, which are fere- doomed to bitter disappointment. A much more drastic measure than that which released the Irish Church from State control will, I venture to think,be carried through the Commons, and that within the lifetime of some of us at any rate. The Clerical Tithe Bill 'is only another proof of the gross immorality of Tory policy in relieving one class of ratepayers at the expense of another. I hope a resolution as strongly framed as you can make it will be passed unanimously condemning the Bill—that latest specimen of injustice, as conspicuous as it is fla- grant, as is nearly every measure a Tory Government introduces into the House of Comrnons.—l am, very truly yours, JOHN FRANCIS MATTHEWS. John Crosfield, Esq. The Rev. W. H. Driimmond wrote :—" I am sorry that I cannot be at the meeting to-morrow night, as I go to London in the morning. My own strong feeling is that the whole question of rural rating ought to be dealt with in a comprehensive way which will affect all alike. These contributions in aid of rates seem to me bad financi. I think we can say this strongly as a matter of principle without saying anything unkind either of the clergy or the Church of England. All re- ligimus animus sh ,ulci certainly be, avoided." Councillor PLIMPTON submitted the following resolu- tion : " That this meeting of the General Council of the Warrington Liberal Association hereby places on record its condemnation of the retrograde policy adopted by her Majesty's Government in the measures taken to afford relief to secti-me of the community from the pub- lic rates, levied upon the reat of the taxpeyera, and especially condemns the latest instance of such class legislation in the Clerical Tithes Bill, now engaging the attention of Parliarpent." (Applause.) It was seconded by Mr. Thomas HAWTHORN, supported by Mr. BIRCHALL and Mr. HAYWARD, and carried unani- mously. Alderman ROBERTS moved, Mr. Bincemr seconded, and the Rev. G. HARBOTTLE supported the second reso- lution, which reads as follows, and which was also carried unanimously :—"Th at copies of the resolution be sent to the right Hon. Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman, M.P., Lord Salisbury, Robert Pierpoint, Esq., M.P., and Colonel Pilkington, M.P." Considerable determination was shiwn to contest the borough at the next election in the Liberal interest. Hopes were held out that the name of a very strong candidate would shortly be submitted to the Committee for approval.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>HALTON'</head>
        <p>HALTON'</p>
        <p>&amp;nous Tic SUCCESS.-Mr. Allan Bazley, son of Mr. Joseph Bazley, farmer, Stockholme, has successfully passed his science examinations at the North Wales University, Bangor, and gained the distinction of B.Sc. Mr. Baz!ey is but 23 years of age and received his elementary education at the hands of Mr. Jeacock, Hal- ton. He ins been resident at Bangor for five years the first three years of which he secured by winning County Council Scholarship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 14, 199 NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JULY 14, 199 NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>A strong team had been got together by Bolton on Saturday to do duty against Warrington on the latter's visit, Albert Ward being inclu led in the eleven. The home team batted first. Thewlis and E. Connor were put on to. bowl for the visitors Against J. L. Rushton and Ellis at the wickets. At Thewhs's secoad ball Hampson caught out Rushton smartly. Ellis give away a chance which was not taken. Scoring was slow for some time fifteen runs being obtained in the next half-bour. More vigorous batting causel a change in the bowling J. W. Bibby and Dr. Smith taking the others places. On Ellis's leg stump being disturbed Ward took his placn. Tee latter, however, only made five runs in the following half-hour owing to the excellent bowling. At 57 the original bowlers resumed and Connor took Stanley's wicket with an excellent ball. The advent of H Owen te partner Ward resulted in Connor's place being taken by Dr. Suaith. The latter succeeded in taking Ward leg before and two runs later Dr. Smith lowered Potter's middle stump. At 93 Bibby took the place of Thewlis. The deputy captain secured two wickets in his first over bowling Stanton, and getting Aspinall caught at square leg. The partnership of Shepherd and Owen was prc- ductiv2 of 36 runs, the former being dismissed with a brilliant hitch, the inning 3 realizing 144. Owen cot:- eluded his innings having scored an invaluable 43.</p>
        <p>The visitors were left an hour and half to save the match. Twenty runs were rattled up in ten minutes by J Callon and The wlis, who were opposed by Owen and Ellis, but four runs later Callon made way for E. Con- nor. Runs were still got at a quick rate but at 37 Connor fell a victim to Ellis, being fairly bate. Dr. Smith joined Thewlis and played superbly, placirg, Ward, who relieved Owen three times in succession for four. Thewlis was bowled for a capital innings of 18 and J. W. Bibby joined his captain, the partnership being profitable,Dr. Smith eventually retiring for a well- played 21. G. Clarke drove to the boundary and was dismissed with the text ball, whilst Knowles was caught at cover with the score at 99. Stumps were then drawn, the game ending in a draw.</p>
        <p>The second teams playel on the Aroley ground some capital batting Ining witnesied. Warrin4ton batted first, and closed their innings for 163 for five wickets. Of this score, C R Adamson made 73, and B Silcock 53, R Crompton earning next with 20. The visitors made the game a draw, losing one wicket for 106, after a fine display of batting.</p>
        <p>At Battersby-lane on Saturday, the Cheshire Lines Engineers and Sankey Wesleyans played a game, the result of which was a win for the Engineers by 18 runs. Watkins for the Wesleyans, and Walker for the Engineers hatted well.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>We are now able to form an idea of the merits of the different clubs in the League. Workingmen's Mission are still at the top dekating Arpley quite easily last week but one, and on Saturday last, beat St. James's on their own ground by the narrow margin of 8 runs. There is every prospect of a tight finish however, for the Post Office are playing very well. Arpley are not at all com- ing up to expectations,ha.ving won one match only since the commencement.Sankey Wire Mills showed a glimpse of their real form by beating the Post Office on Saturday and should occupy a good position at the finish. Rich- monds have only played three matches as yet winning two and losing the other and may finish well. Two of the other clubs have through the weather bad to share the points. St. James's seem to be pursued by ill-luck, it being rare for them to play with a full team, a fact which has been of advantage to other clubs. Appended is the latest table</p>
        <p>P W L D Apts Workingmen's Mission—. 6 ... 4 ... 1 1 9 Post Office Sankey Wire Mills Riehmonds Arpley St. James's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WIDNES ATHLETIC CLUB.</head>
        <p>WIDNES ATHLETIC CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual athletic festival was held on Friday and Saturday, on the ground of the Widnes Feotball Club. The weather was all that could be desired, and the attendance was excellent. Pr;zes to the value of £250 were offered. Results :</p>
        <p>120 Y Ards Flat Race Handicap (District).—Winners of bests , W Wellings. Farnworth Harriers, l yards ; T Horan,Warring‘on F,C., scratch; E Hodgekinson,Warrington, 4 yards and C H Spelman, Prescot 2 yards. Final 1, W Wellings ; 2, C H Spelman ; 3, T Horan. Time, 12 1-5 sec,nds, splendid race, won by inches, half a foot between second and third.</p>
        <p>Two Miles B:cycle Race Handicap (District).—First heat : 1, T B Tickle, St Helens OC. 50 yards; 2, H 0 Jones, Liverpool Wheelers, 80 yards ; 3. T Singleton, Clarence. 110 yards. Second heat 1, F A Grainger, Northwieh CC. 35 y ras ; 2, W Collin, W P RC. 20 yards ; 3, A T Daintith, Warrington C 0, 70 yards. Find : 1, r It Tickle ;2, A T Daintith ; 3. ii C Jones. The pace was poor until the last two laps, when Daintith put on a spurt, but was beaten by half a wheel, seven yards dividing second and third. Time, smin, 51 I's sec.</p>
        <p>Half-Mile Flat Race Handicap (confined to the employees of the United Alkali Company, Limited)--1, Harry Lawton, Gaskell Deacon Works, 30 yards ; 2, Fred Peters, Pilkington Works, 45 yards ; 3, G Wright. Golding Davis' Work, 15 yards. Won by two yards ; twelve yards bAwern second and third. Time, 2 min. 3 sec. 200 Yards Scholars' Race Handicap.—First heat : 1, W Joseph Dennett, St Bede's, 35 yards ; 2, William Houghton, Warrington-road Board, scratch. Second beat 1, Thomas Balmer, Widnes National ; eleven yards ;2. Alexander Flynn, St Bede's 35 yards. Third heat : 1, Joseph Dixon. Widnes National, 9 yards; 2, Stanley Monks, Farnworth Grammar, 6 yards. Fourth heat : 1. Albert Hunt, West Bank Board, 13 yards; 2, Oliver Twist, Widnes National, 25 yards. Fifth heat : 1, Albert Hollingworth, Warrington-road Board yards ; Edward Connolly, St Bede's 20 yards. Final,: 1, 8 Monks ;2, J Dixon ;3, W Houghton. Won by half a yard ; second and third close together. Time, 4 min 31 2-ssecs. One Mile Flat Rise Handicap (District) —l, James F Meharry, Liverpool Xaviers, 40 yards ' • 2, W 8 Collett Sefton Harriers, 45 yards ; 3. M Folion, Farnworth Harriers, 55 yards. Time. 4min 31 2-ssecs. Five Miles BicyJle Race, scratch, Widnes Cycling Club Championship.—l. Walter Sherratt ; 2, NVilliam F Popplo; 3, Thomas Harrison. Sherratt won in splendid style, and this being the third year he has carried off the championship, the silver challenge cup now becomes his property. SATURDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The sports were continued on Saturday and resulted as follows--</p>
        <p>120 Yards Flat Race Handicap (Open).—Winners of heats : P A Percival. Newton-le•Willows, 8:1 yards ; James Clarke, Blundellsands, 9t yards ; W Wettings, Farnworth Harriers, 9i yards ;J 13 Cowan, Manchester Harriers, 8 yards ; ii sic- Oraken, Fairfield, 12 yards ; Herbert Kent, Liverpool, 101 yards; J Smith, Bohemians AC, 12 yards; Jahn Saunders, Sefton Harriers. 114 yards ;J A 1 Cadman, Salford Harriers, 10L- yards ;D C Western. Liverpool H and A C 114 yards ; T Horan, Warrington P 0, 84 yards ; H H Fleetwood, Sefton Harriers, 114 yards ; 13 G Benson, Sefton Harriers, 114 yards; Decicting heat : 1, J Saunders ; 2, J Clarke: 3, G Adams. Time 114 seconds. This was a grand race, inches only separating the first two, and a foot between second and Third.</p>
        <p>Three Miles Bicycle Race Handicap (Open) : 1. T R Tickle, St. Helens C.C., 80 yards ; 2, S McKevitt. Liver- pool, 180 yards; 3, E E Woods, Warrington, 190 yards. At the last lap, Tickle got awaj, and won easily. A length separated the second and third. Turning the lost corner, Ind, who seemed a certain second, came a cropper, and spoiled Daintith's chance as well. One Mile Flat Race Handicap (Open) : 1, F Supplies, Liverpool, 115 yards ; 2, W Mason, Sheffield Ignited Harriers, 90 yards ; 3, A Douglas Mather, Farriworth Harriers, 125 yards. Won by three yards, twenty yards between second and third. Time, 4mins. 18secs. Tug of War, eight men (Open).-In the final,Bootle Police, captain Luke Mansell, beat Sefton Athletic and 0 C, captain II L Jones. In the first round, at the very first pull, the rope which had been supplied broke, and some time elapsed ere another could be obtained, The Bootle Police had matters all their own way. One Mile Bicycle Race Handicap (Open).-First heat 1, E Woods, Warrington, 19) yards ; 2, F A Grainger,North- wieh OC, 120 yards; 3, J Done. Chester OC, 179 yards. Second beat : 1, S McKevitt. Liverpool, 185 yards ; 2, J Gudgeon, Northwieh C C, 150 yards; 3, 0 A Cowie, Chester CO, 175 yards. Third heat: 1, T R Tickle, St Helens CC. 135 yards; 2, W J Balshaw. St Helens C C, 185 yards ;3, L Myerscongh, Thornton, AA C, 195 yards. Deciding heat 1, 13 E Woods; 2, L Myerscough ; 3, 8 McKevitt. Woods maintained his lead all the way and won by 8 yards, the same space separating second and third. IgHalf-Mile Flat Race Handicap (Open). J T Bouch, Pam- worth Harriers. Harriers. 55 yards; 2, B Heap. M AC. 55 yards ;3. D Wash. Liverpool Harriers, 51 yards. A good race was won by a yard. Time 2 minutes. 100 Yards Flat Race Scratch (Open.)--1, R W Wadsley, Highgate Harriers, 100 yards and 1-mile champion ; 2,George Bevins Bootle ; 3, 0 H Spehnan, Prescot. Wadsley won with ease by three yards. Time. 1 2-ssecs. Quarter-Mile Flat Race Handicap (Open.)-Winners of heats. First heat : 1, T E Holland, Fairfield. 35 yards ;2. Bryan, Prescot, 35 yards. Second heat 1, Albert Abel Runcorn, 29 yards ; 2, T Williams, Wavertree, 30 yards. Third heat : 1. James Sage, Sefton Harriers, 25 yards ;2, G Griffiths, Liverpool Harriers, 28 yards. Fourth;heat : 1, Joseph Thomas Carling, Se on Harriers, 28 yards ; 2, Arthur R Thomas, Sefton Harriers. 30 yards. Final : 1, A Abel ;2, T E Holland ;3, G Griffiths. Thus was one of the best races run inches only separating the first three. Two Miles Inter-Club Race Over Hurdles.-The race was a splendid sight, the hurdles being taken in grand style. The first man in was J T Rinarner (N C A A two miles champion) of the Liverpool Harriers, Salford Harriers, the northern champions, won with twe've points, Manchester second with 25 points and West Cheshire third with 27 points.</p>
        <p>TRE " EXAMINER " WORKS. BEWSEY STREET.</p>
