        <p>£4,000,000 HAVE NOW BEEN PAID IN RESPECT OF RAILWAY ACCIDENTS, ALL ACCIDENTS, WORKMEN'S ACCIDENTS FIDELITY GUARANTEE. BY THE RAIL WAY PASSENGERS' ASSURANCE Co. 64, CORNHILL, LONDON. A. VIAN, Secretary. AGENTS required in unrepresented Districts. Manchester Office : 64, Cross Street. AGENTS: Warrington Mr. C. T. HOLMES, Market Gate „ Mr. W.H. RlGBRY.Nesscliffe, Victoria-rd.,Latchford St. Helens Mr. J. B. LEACH, Hardshaw-street Kuutsford Mr. ALFRED E. CUTTER Sandbach Mr. F.J. FINLOW, Rookery Bridge Fan2wortla Mr. I. HOLLAND WATSON,Hoertleld House</p>
        <p>BRITISH SHIPS WRECKED. SERIOUS LOSS OF LIFE. The Press Association has received the following cablegram through Lloyd's agent at Fremantle, Western Australia :—" The British ship, Carlisle Castle, bound from the Cylde for Fremantle, has been totally lost off Rockingham." A Renter's telegram from Perth, West Australia, says all hands are lost. The wrecked vessel was built at Blackwell in 1868, and carried nearly a couple of thousand tons general cargo. She had ari skipper Captain Lindsey,ancl was manned by an English crew of 21 hands. Sho left the Clyde on March 21. A Reuter's telegram reports that all hands were lost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-07-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>-WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>-WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>rho annual general meetingo tha Warrington Musical Society was held in the Trinity Sch)olrcom, Museum-street, on Tuesday the chair being taken by the president Mr. J. Charlton Parr. Thera was a moderate attendance of members.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Anus the hon. secretary in reporting on the work of the season just ended said that the society's prospectus showed no change from the previous season in the number of concerts given.and no differetace in the priceslof admission and subrcriptions.viz ,three subscrip- tion concerts,and one extra concert at popular prices. On Wednesday, November 9 the first subscription was given when the programme included Haydn's " Creation " (parts 1 and 2) and a miscellaneme second laa,f. The soloists engaged were. Miss Evangeline Florence, Mr. William Green, and Mr. Andrew Black. There seemed to be something of the impossible in actually getting Mr. William Green to appear before a Warrington audience, and he was sorry to say that this proved to be the second occasion when the society bad to obtain a sub- stitute at the last minute. Be sincerely believed th at 1 Mr. Green was not well enough to attend, and he , underetood that Mr. Green suffered most unfortunately from a delicate throat. Much as the committee disliked any alteration of the artistes announced, they had to submit, and Mr. E iward Branscombe proved himself a most efficient substitute. They were always liable to an upset of their plans.and:the last few hours before a con- cert, was always an anxious:time. He was glad to sty that there were no other mishaps cf this kind to report. The practice ef giving a performance of "The Messiah" at popular prices on a Saturday evening about Christ- mas time had been adhered to, and at the performance of the oratorio on December 17th the solo vocalists were Madame Laura Haworth, Miss Juanita Jones, Mr. Samuel Masters, and Mr. Charles Phillips. The second subscription concert wal given on Wednesday, February Bth, wnen the programme included a work which he thought entirely new to Warrington—Stanford's "The Revenge." Madame Ella Russell was the vocalist engaged and, theta&amp; it was not in his province to criti- cise in any way, he understood that the concert was as much enjoyed and appreciated as any the society had given. Mr. Crossley had been instrumental in helping them to hear the seciety's organ on that occasion in a way they had previously had no opportunity of doing, by inducing Dr. Reynolds to include a concerto and one or two organ solos in the programme, and he thought the society would not hesitate to repeat tie invitation to Dr. Reynolds at a not distant date. The Trombone quartet party made up the list of artietee engaged and the part-song by the chorus "You stole my love " (Macfarren) the " The Soldiers Chorus" from Faust with items for the orchestra alone and the selendidly rendered solos by Madame Russell went to complete a capital programme. The third sabecription and final concert of the past season was the performance of Mendelssehn's grand oratorio"St Paul" on Wednesday March 22ad, when Madame Clara Sanueel, Mr. Gwilyin Richards and Mr. Bantocle Pierpoint were the principal vocalists. At this concert they were again helped in the solo parts by members of the chorus and the society's thanks were due and he knew would b i most freely offered to Miss Jeans and to Mr. Eccles for the help they Lad given. (Cheers.) Speaking of the work of the members it was noticeable that there appeared to be a slight dread at first of the rehearsals of "The Revenge" and he thought Mr. Crossley would agree with him that there was too often a preference shewn on the part of the members for what he might call known works. It would he a pity if they could not enter with spirit into an attack on some new works rather than go on repeating certain oratorios over and over again. He knew that he was expressing the feelings of their en- thusiastic and energetic conductor when he expressed the desire he felt that the Musical Society should do 1 more than this, The Society, as a whole, showed too much nervousness about advancing into unknown ground and he thought that was was quite needless as the performance of "The Revenge" showed. There was of course an obstacle in the way of performing many of the works by the more modern composers which wa, more difficulty to overcome than the want of nerve b, choir or band. He alluded to the want of funds. Wh' Rot undertake "The Golden Lsgend "? had been aseede 1 over and over again by outsiders. The answer was that so long as they bad no funds in hand to fall back upon " The Golden Legend "and other excellent works were a sort of forbidden fruit to them. The successful season of 1897-8 had been most diffcult to follow, and the com- mittee in decidiag on the programme of the season just ended had exercised all reasonable economy as would be Seen from the hon. treasurer's report. Coming to the facts and figures relating to the season's work there had been in all 38 rehearsals, the final one being on March 21st last. The numerical strength of the society had been well main- tained. The members on the books numbered 63 sopranos, 59 contraltos, 29 tenor, 39 basses, and 39 orchestra, making a total of 227. In the attendance at rehearsals there had b.en little to complain of, on the ceatrary there was possibly an increase in the total attendance towards the end of the season as compared with pact years. The committee had worked well and amicably and his thanks were in this connection due to eeveral fellow-officers of the society for the kind way in which they had assisted to make his task a pleasant one during the season of 1898-9. Mr. Atkin proceeded te express the deep regret with which he had to record the loss of one who, in addition to being an ardent talented and enthusiastic musician, was both a fellow- townsman and one who closely allied himself with the eociety,and was moreover a kind and generous friend to them at all times. He felt bound to say that by the sad death of Mr. Frank Reynolds they had not only lost—many of them, a dear personal friend—hut all as a society, one whose kindly and sincere sympathy and deep interests in their efforts it would be impossible to forget, as long as the society ex- isted. (Hear, hear). Mr. le REYNOLDS (the society hon. treasurer, then proceeded to deal with the society's accounts, from which it was shown that there had been a loss of £3 is 91 on the season's work, the tot al receipts being £403 us 3d and the expenditure £4ll 13s 01. Too society's d eot which stood at £49 us 7d, was thus increased te £52 135 4d.</p>
        <p>Mt. PARR in submitting the two reports to the meet- ing, thought that on the whole, there was little to be discouraged aleeut in the year's work of the Society. Against the small adverse bal- ance, must be considered the pleasure that had been given to the inhabitants of the town and the educational effect of the concerts from a musical point of view. There had he also thought been some- thing like a plethora of concerts in the town during the past winter end it was therefore not surprising that the society's finances were consequently affected in the way shown by the accounts. The election of officers was then proceeded with the president and vice-presidonts being unanimously re- elected with the addition amongst the latter of Dr. White. The whole of the committee and officers as dur- ing the past season were also voted upon and re-elected. Votes of thanes being heartily accorded to the hon. secretary, the hon. treasurer, and others bearing the burden of the management of the society's affairs. Responding to a vote of thanks to the society's con- ductor, proposed by Mr. Parr, Mr. F. H. CROSSLEY touched upon the work clone by the slclety during the past season and expressed the deep interest ho felt in his duties as its conductor. As to the small adverse balance on the season's concerts he did not think they need re- garcifthat very seriously. Indeed he would remind that it bad aeparently been lost sight of that in the pur- chase during the pest year of new music-stands for the orchestra and also of pieces of music for performance they had an asset which more than covered the deficit. As to the music for the approaching season the committee bad already tentatively considereda pro- gramme which if it were carried out would to some extent realise hi 3 wish that they should, do something beyond reproducing time after time the standard oratorios. Of course there were difficulties in the way of attemptine new works. A new work by an unknown composer would not draw an audience together. But on the other side they had the interests of the members themselev3 to consider and the objeets for which they met together as a society. The slight feeling of revolt which had been noticed at a work such as " The Revenge " ought not to exist. It was a little dishearten- ing that the members should take an interest only in works which they knew and liked. One of committees, greatest difficulties was thus the selecting of a suitable programme and he was glad to say that they proposed Ito make a new departure. For the first concert of next season it was intended to give a concert per- formance of Gounod's opera " Faust " and "The 'Messiah" at Christmas as usual. Then at a miscellaneous concert early, in the new year, it was proposed to in- clude Sullivans " On Shore and Sea," which had not been beard since it was given at the openine of the Parr Hall, and for the last concert Goemod's " Redemp- tion " had been thought ef as a most appropriate work for the lenten season. (Cheei4). Be had himself been much struck with the b. auties of this work, and its suit- ability, whilst engaged on its rehearsal and performance at the Liverpool society, which he conducted. At the conclusion of the meeting Mr. RICHARD FAIRCLOUGH raised the question of the finances relating to the society's organ in Perr Hall, and proposed that in future the figures should be laid before the annual meeting of the society. It was announced by Mr: REYNOLDS that there was still a debt of £6l. 133 31 on the organ, towards which could be planed a sum of £2O which would be due for the rent of the instrument from the Parr Hall Commit- tee later on in the year. A cordial vote of thanks to Mr. Parr for presiding, proposed by Dr. White in felicitous terms, concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON EXAMINER,</p>
