        <head>ANOTHER DISASTER.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER DISASTER.</p>
        <p>PERTH, WEST AUSTRALIA, THURSDAY. During a storm on the c3ast yesterday the City of York, bound f rom San Francisco for Freemantle, waø wrecked off Rottnest Island. The captain and eleven men ot her crew are missing. Seven others have been saved.</p>
        <p>LADY SALISBURY SEIZED WITH PARALYSIS,</p>
        <p>The Exchange Telegraph Company, is officially in- formed that Lady Salisbury has bad a slight attack of paralysis, from which shs has rallied well, and is going on satisfactorily.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY GUARD CUT TO PIECES. Shortly before eleven o'clock yesterday morning, a goods guard, named William Abell, in the employ of the Midland Railway Company, was cut to pieces by a bal- last train in the sidings at the gasworks at Buxton. De- ceased stepped off his own train into the six-footway, and failed to see the other train approaching. &amp;belt leaves a widow and family.</p>
        <p>ARRIVAL OF AMERICAN ATHLETES.</p>
        <p>The American line steamer St. Louis, whlClT—rea-c—hed Southampton on Wednesday night, brought the picked athletes of Yale and Harvard Universities who are to meet the Oxford and Cambridge champions.</p>
        <p>SUICIDE OF A BREWER'S SECRETARY. At noon, yesterday, at his residence, Alfred-street, Bury, Mr. Charles Davenport secretary of the Brewery company, Limited, Bury, committed suicide by shoot— g himself with a revolver. He was thirty-two years of age. This morning he complained of pains-in his head. No reason can be assigned at present for his rash act, and the whole affair at present is shrouded in mystery. He was well known in brewery circles, and the affair has created intense excitement.</p>
        <p>LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST. The weather torecast for the North-west of England and North Wales up to noon to-day is as follows : Southerly to south-westerly winds ; moderate or fresh ; fair at first, some rain later. JOHN BRIGGS.</p>
        <p>A telegram says yesterday's bulletin from Cheadle Asylum, whither Brigs has beoa conveyed, is that, though quieter, his excitement is still considerable. Till he is calmer, the doctors cannot indicate the exact pro- babilities of the future.</p>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S CRTOKET. CLOSE OF PLAY SCORES. LANCASHIRE v. DERBYSHIRE :—Lancasbire 132 ; Derbyshire 56 ; Lancashire 2nd innings, 32 for 1. AUSTRALIANS v. GLOUCESTERSHIRE :—Austra• Hans, all out, 377. WARWICKSHIRE v. YOKKSHIRE :—Yorhshire 350 for 6.</p>
        <p>ESSEX v SURREY :—Essex 37; Surrey 146; Esseg 2nd inning., 83 for 6. NOTTS v. SUSSEX :—Notts, 293 fcr 4.</p>
