        <p>RUNCORN HEATH AND COMMON. MR. J. WILDING'S SCHEME APPROVED. On good authority 97P are informed that at the com- mittee meeting of the Runcorn Urban District Council, held at the Town Hall on Thursday morning, Mr. James Wilcling!(surveyor) submitted a sketch and draft scheme of the pro;)oed conversion of the Heath and Common into a park and pleasure ground for the inhabitants of Runcorn,which with certain reservations it was decided to adopt and forward to the Council meeting for ap- proval. The scheme shows that the extent of the ground at disposal is about 22 acres. The triangular portion at the top end of the common, about 8 acres in extent, is to be the recreation ground and will be at the disposal of cricket and such like games. The remaining portion, with the exception of about two acres—the site of a proposed lake—will be converted into a park and planted with shrubs, flower beds, etc., interspersed with gravel footpaths, along which will be distributed the customary " park benches." The lake it is intended should be a uniform depth of about two feet and in summer will be at the disposal of the "youngster yatchman" whose minia- ture cutter and battleship may plough its waters without let or hindrance. During the winter naturally safe skating will be provided every time " King Frost " feels disposed to be agreeable. The cost of the whole is esti- mated at about id. in the £, for 30 years, after which there be will but the cost of maintenance. The whole scheme commends itself to the Council and it is to be hoped will go through the hands of that august body speedily.</p>
